+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012rUZ9x3fcsyXDtMY4rGTUb","type":"tool_result","content":"91568\t#### D.110c — blocking repeated same-room authenticated epoch lifecycle\n91569\t\n91570\t##### Inherited evidence, boundary, owner, and deadline\n91571\t\n91572\tD.110c preserves D.108, D.109, and D.110a exactly as accepted. D.108 proves the\n91573\tfirst genuine creator transition from epoch 0 to epoch 1. D.109f proves a\n91574\t128-step synthetic durable-maintenance differential plus one genuine lifecycle;\n91575\tit explicitly does not claim repeated installed-v3 rollover. D.110a proves\n91576\tdistinct-room churn, per-handoff reclamation, exact million-operation accounting,\n91577\tand bounded retained memory across 64 rooms that each close only once. None is\n91578\treopened, rerun, weakened, or relabelled as repeated epochs of one room.\n91579\t\n91580\tD.110c is a blocking coordinated production-lifecycle umbrella owned by the\n91581\t`@ts-drp/node` installed-v3 creator close/adoption/reopen orchestrators, with the\n91582\texisting `@ts-drp/protocol-v3`, compaction, storage, issuance, snapshot,\n91583\tavailability, archive, and runtime-reclamation owners retained as verification\n91584\tboundaries. Its deadline is before Phase-6 exit, before D.110b or any later gate\n91585\tis used to claim bounded memory _after E epochs_, and before Phase 7a/7b or either\n91586\tgolden path consumes a long-lived-room claim. D.110b's CI trend wiring may not\n91587\terase or satisfy this blocker.\n91588\t\n91589\tThis checkpoint is audit and plan only. It authorizes no production edit, RED,\n91590\tcampaign, wire-format change, authority change, dependency change, threshold\n91591\tchange, public API, or Phase-7 implementation. The bounded source audit below\n91592\tdemonstrates that the gap is not merely test composition or one private Node\n91593\torchestration bug. It crosses an exported result contract, creator seal\n91594\tauthority/evidence custody, bounded authenticated trust recovery, product-room\n91595\townership, and durable transition recovery. Those owners are explicitly\n91596\tresliced before any production change.\n91597\t\n91598\t##### Bounded source and architecture audit\n91599\t\n91600\tSome close primitives are epoch-relative, but the complete production path is\n91601\tnot. `prepareCreatorClose` derives the cut epoch from authenticated\n91602\t`currentTrust`, enforces exact history extension, and binds the successor\n91603\tanchor. By contrast, creator seal custody and several Node/product owners are\n91604\tstill epoch-0/first-successor-only:\n91605\t\n91606\t| Lifecycle concern                                        | Current production path                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | Exact repeated-epoch gap and required custody                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n91607\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n91608\t| Live issue and publication                               | `v3-live.ts` activates the adopted successor as an ordinary non-`snapshot-closed` plane; `issueLocal`, pending/retained publication, ingress, and stable topic use the recovered epoch and anchor.                                                                                                                                                                                            | The live path already works at epoch 1. Preserve it and prove the first post-epoch-2 issue, publication, ingress, journal row, reducer state, and digest rather than adding another issuing API.                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n91609\t| Close set and compact history                            | `creatorCloseRegistration` captures the genuine graph and authenticated anchor; `creator-close.ts` calls `deriveCloseSetHistoryCommitment`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | The close owner always supplies a new empty accumulator. Epoch 1 commits nonzero prior history, so the genuine second close fails exact accumulator/root/size verification. The adopted projection retains `compactHistory`, but the live registration neither authenticates nor carries one shared continuity owner.                                                                           |\n91610\t| Creator seal and QC authority                            | `openSealAuthority` in protocol-v3 refuses any creator trust whose `currentEpoch !== 0`; protocol seal authority, proposal, vote, and QC identities bind literal epoch 0. `@ts-drp/seal` creator intent, vote intent, pacemaker, evidence, vote stores, and browser adapters also carry epoch-0 literals, and the singleton evidence actor cannot reuse an epoch-0 finalized row for epoch 1. | No genuine epoch-1 cut can be certified or verified today. Generalizing creator-only seal custody and selecting evidence/vote custody by authenticated `(objectId, closedEpoch)` is a security-authority and durable-record contract, not a Node-only fix. Certified/BFT epoch-zero behavior and all existing vectors remain unchanged.                                                         |\n91611\t| Exported close result                                    | `@ts-drp/node/creator-close` publicly exports `CreatorLiveCloseResult` with literal `epoch: 0` and `successorEpoch: 1`; the runtime returns the same constants.                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Repeated close requires one reviewed public-contract generalization to safe integer epochs with exact `successorEpoch === epoch + 1`. Field names and result shape remain unchanged; this is still a public API change and is never hidden as private plumbing.                                                                                                                                 |\n91612\t| Snapshot and manifest                                    | Snapshot staging/export/quarantine bind the registration's current object, epoch, anchor, ACL, and state digest.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | The existing format appears epoch-relative. Prove epoch-N export and epoch-N+1 verification cannot substitute an earlier snapshot; stop if a format change is demonstrated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n91613\t| Next anchor, projection, and adoption                    | `creator-adoption.ts` verifies the first history advance and emits `v3-live-generation-2`, while hot verify/commit code requires predecessor kind `v3-live-generation-1` and constructs epoch 1.                                                                                                                                                                                              | Generalize existing epoch fields and choose predecessor kind from authenticated epoch. Do not add a new projection kind or fixture-authored capability.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n91614\t| Activation and product custody                           | Adoption uses one-use capabilities and strict CAS. `creator-adoption-activate.ts` keys `activeOwners` only by stable topic and returns the existing wrapper when bindings match. `examples/v3-room` binds close only for genesis, treats a set successor authority as permanently adopted, and does not bind close after cold reopen.                                                         | A second adoption can report success while returning the stale epoch-1 wrapper. The product owner must replace ownership only for the exact next authenticated anchor, rebind close to every adopted/reopened successor, and preserve the stale-handle refusal contract.                                                                                                                        |\n91615\t| Cold reopen and trust root                               | Reopen assumes the first three-generation shape, genesis projection, one cut/QC, epoch-1 successor, and the genesis-only trust opener. `inspectTrustClosure` permits exactly one trust record and every trust advance removes the prior record.                                                                                                                                               | After repeated D.109 pruning, no bounded authenticated carrier currently proves an arbitrary current trust from the pinned genesis. Replaying an ever-growing cut/QC chain or retaining every old trust record would violate age-independent recovery or bounded durable-control state. A reviewed trust-checkpoint/control-proof compaction design is required before implementation.          |\n91616\t| Durable control-plane census                             | `exactCombinedClosure` retains every old cut and commit-QC ref while replacing only the trust record; adoption also retains predecessor ACL material.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | The current active closure grows linearly with epochs. The user's bounded durable-structure gate may not be weakened to accept exact linear `f(N)`. The checkpoint design must bound hot/control closure and recovery state while accounting archive/history data separately. Any change to closure law, trust opening, or authenticated checkpoint bytes is high-risk and separately reviewed. |\n91617\t| Closed-but-unadopted restart                             | Verified adoption facts are attached to the in-process close handle; no genuine cross-process entry reconstructs and resumes a durable closed/pending-adoption state.                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | The required post-close and pre-adoption restart gates need a named durable resume capability. If it requires a new public API or authority carrier, that change is its own reviewed prerequisite; the gate is not dropped or relabelled as in-process recovery.                                                                                                                                |\n91618\t| Offline/rebase outbox                                    | Successor activation carries the issuance store/scope and live journal, reconstructs displaced-source authority, and exposes rebase completion.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Prove no pending/published row is lost, duplicated, reassigned, or accepted under a stale epoch; preserve the issuance watermark and first valid post-reopen publication.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n91619\t| Rollback, availability, pruning, and runtime reclamation | D.109 planning is epoch-numeric and deletes only after verified adoption, exact head, two rollback generations, policy, snapshot, and categorized outbox. D.109d binds `closedEpoch` to successor `closedEpoch + 1`.                                                                                                                                                                          | Reuse these owners only with authentic per-transition facts. Prove sequential prune/catch-up, distinct receipts, idempotent replay, two rollback generations, and bounded live predecessor/registration custody. A failure may retain too much but never delete too much.                                                                                                                       |\n91620\t| Archive and cold paging                                  | The current anchor/cut schemas carry `archiveIndexRoot`, but protocol-v3 creator close requires the next input root to equal the current anchor root. Phase 7 archive segmentation/paging is not implemented in the production packages.                                                                                                                                                      | D.110c can supply a genuine multi-epoch producer and preserve an existing root, but cannot silently evolve archive lineage or cold-page messages. Evolving the committed archive root is a separate Phase-7 wire/authority reslice. The Discord golden path stays blocked until that downstream slice consumes D.110c output.                                                                   |\n91621\t\n91622\tThe genuine second close therefore has several ordered failures, not one: an\n91623\tepoch-1 seal actor cannot open or certify a QC; the close history owner has only\n91624\tan empty prior accumulator; the exported close result and adoption path encode\n91625\t0→1; activation can return the stale topic owner; and restart/pruning have no\n91626\tbounded authenticated current-trust checkpoint. D.110c is a coordinated\n91627\tproduct/security lifecycle program. It already demonstrates a need for a\n91628\tbounded public result-contract change and separate security-authority,\n91629\tdurable-checkpoint, control-closure, and resume decisions. That is why this\n91630\tcheckpoint stops at audit/reslice. Any registry kind/domain, wire field,\n91631\tdependency, workload, threshold, availability/deletion rule, or additional\n91632\tpublic surface beyond the explicitly reviewed owners below stops the affected\n91633\tsub-slice for another high-risk reslice.\n91634\t\n91635\t##### D.110c-0b Hedera/Hiero comparative authority-lineage audit\n91636\t\n91637\tThis bounded comparison is design evidence, not an implementation dependency or\n91638\tauthorization. The audit pinned `hiero-improvement-proposals` at commit\n91639\t[`54ccb06659592ab201e7adea632f1019e9faa00e`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/tree/54ccb06659592ab201e7adea632f1019e9faa00e)\n91640\t(tree `42ff7d2c1ff68ef662b405cb167838849d1b49f1`) and HIP-1200 blob\n91641\t`088088185786375a1478166bbd61c4021eedc85c`; it pinned\n91642\t`hiero-consensus-node` at commit\n91643\t[`1aa1d6c153907750cfbba6935b7a21867053968e`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/tree/1aa1d6c153907750cfbba6935b7a21867053968e)\n91644\t(tree `7c7adb3015c782e4131cc2ad1ca7ec5f97cb8cc5`). The inspected\n91645\texact-weight design blob is `c35cb34e6e797719fdb02f8541cb067f64e3972a`;\n91646\tthe implementation blobs for `HistoryLibrary`, `HistoryLibraryImpl`,\n91647\t`WrapsHistoryProver`, `WritableHistoryStoreImpl`, `V071HistorySchema`,\n91648\t`TssHandoffCoordinator`, `WrapsProvingKeyVerification`, and\n91649\t`history_types.proto` are respectively\n91650\t`c4c998d06d611fe7ece9c62346fb6fad4c7a671c`,\n91651\t`c53deacbffe3352a0dba74d46cc7ddf265f4ed6c`,\n91652\t`fc7c893b20b9246be8f2b814af7810500ce4fa2a`,\n91653\t`589537503db6da008b876b540c37664cbb9665ba`,\n91654\t`0cb24998101688d3fe0f09986cceea780fd213d4`,\n91655\t`8ca7f9a87c853663add1bfb194b6017a80c6a2ff`,\n91656\t`e15e6c352a085d3d44b3ac433712b6dfcc070647`, and\n91657\t`5b4d59d4002426af096423af879390f883eda1ff`. The pinned test blobs for\n91658\thistory-store handoff/purge, WRAPS handoff, reconnect reset, and signed-state\n91659\tvalidation are also retained in the audit ledger. The native implementation was\n91660\tindependently pinned at `hiero-cryptography` commit\n91661\t[`39f28f39f609f80e52253d86169e2db5216a713e`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-cryptography/tree/39f28f39f609f80e52253d86169e2db5216a713e),\n91662\twith WRAPS `lib.rs`/Cargo blobs\n91663\t`5beccdf8fdf35a5da6f55112663faa1b829bd849` and\n91664\t`f5aba82bedd5d17b8127e211ecf3d97632eb0a5a`. These pins distinguish\n91665\twhat was inspected from later upstream movement.\n91666\t\n91667\tHIP-1200 is an approved proposal that defines a stable ledger ID as the hash of\n91668\tthe genesis TSS address book and a chain-of-trust proof for a current history.\n91669\tThe pinned consensus source demonstrates that `HistoryService`, genesis and\n91670\tincremental WRAPS construction, compressed-proof verification, persisted\n91671\tactive/next constructions, joint Hints/History handoff, reconnect reset, and\n91672\thandoff purging are implemented on that revision. Source and tests do **not** by\n91673\tthemselves prove a specific public-network deployment or operational enablement;\n91674\tthis audit makes no such claim. In particular, the pinned `TssConfig` still\n91675\tdefaults `forceMockSignatures` to true pending a full block-stream cutover. The\n91676\texact-weight document is architecture documentation. HIP-1200 describes packed\n91677\tblock-signature sizes and notes that ArkWorks/snarkjs compatibility is\n91678\tunconfirmed; the implemented protobuf uses one opaque `block_signature` byte\n91679\tfield. Proposal sizes/layout and implementation defaults are recorded\n91680\tseparately and are not ts-drp acceptance constants.\n91681\t\n91682\tThe transferable comparison is deliberately narrow:\n91683\t\n91684\t| Hiero concept                   | ts-drp analogue                                 | Important non-equivalence                                                                                                                             |\n91685\t| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n91686\t| Genesis roster hash / ledger ID | Pinned room genesis anchor                      | A network-wide roster root is not a per-room creator key, and genesis authenticity remains out-of-band in both systems.                               |\n91687\t| Evolving roster and proof keys  | Evolving room ACL/authority                     | In D.110c's `creator-trusted-v1` scope, the creator checkpoint signer/profile/signer set stays fixed while the signed anchor's ACL digest may evolve. |\n91688\t| Roster transition and handoff   | Authenticated epoch close, adoption, activation | Hiero coordinates two services and a roster; ts-drp coordinates cut/QC, snapshot, trust, product ownership, and cleanup.                              |\n91689\t| Threshold signature             | Cut/QC certification                            | Hiero uses exact weights; creator-only ts-drp has one trusted signer and must not be relabelled BFT.                                                  |\n91690\t| Recursive WRAPS proof           | Candidate compact authority-lineage proof       | It is a design family only; no WRAPS, SNARK, BLS/hinTS, CRS, or proving artifact enters ts-drp implicitly.                                            |\n91691\t| Current signed state/history    | Authenticated room trust/checkpoint state       | A valid signed checkpoint establishes authenticity, not freshness against withholding or replay by itself.                                            |\n91692\t| Block/mirror history            | Archived room epochs/messages                   | Historical application bytes are separately growing archive state, not active authority/control proof.                                                |\n91693\t\n91694\tThe pinned Hiero construction establishes the following and no more. A verifier\n91695\tauthenticates the ledger ID out of band and verifies a compressed recursive\n91696\tproof whose initial state binds the genesis address-book hash and whose final\n91697\tstate binds the Hints verification-key hash. The inspected compressed verifier\n91698\tdoes **not** separately bind the final roster hash, transition count, or\n91699\tfreshness. Current-roster selection and freshness remain obligations of the\n91700\tsurrounding signed-state/block-number and caller-held-floor owners, not\n91701\tproperties inherited from WRAPS. Genesis starts with aggregate Schnorr signatures;\n91702\teach incremental construction witnesses the prior uncompressed recursive proof,\n91703\tthe source address book/proof keys, the target address book and verification\n91704\tkey, and source-roster authorization of that transition. The Rust recursive\n91705\tcircuit enforces strictly more than one-half of the preceding roster's weight\n91706\tfor the aggregate Schnorr authorization. The separate off-circuit\n91707\trecursive-proof vote-acceptance threshold is at least one-third of source\n91708\tweight, while target key readiness is strictly greater than two-thirds.\n91709\tHIP-1200 itself uses both the greater-than-one-half and one-third descriptions\n91710\tin different sections. Those distinct proof, vote-acceptance, and liveness\n91711\tthresholds must not be collapsed into one claim or imported into ts-drp's\n91712\tquorum law. Hiero reconnect freshness is likewise external to WRAPS:\n91713\t`DefaultSignedStateValidator.throwIfOld` compares learned state with the\n91714\tlearner's caller-held prior round/timestamp floor.\n91715\t\n91716\tThe externally presented recursive proof and its verification cost are\n91717\tage-independent, but the complete system is not stateless. It retains the\n91718\tledger ID, an active construction, at most one next construction, current proof\n91719\tkeys, construction-scoped votes/messages while in flight, the target history,\n91720\tand an uncompressed WRAPS proof needed to extend recursion. The prover also\n91721\trequires four separately installed, hash-verified proving/verification artifact\n91722\tfiles. The pinned native Rust/JNI implementation uses Nova folding with a\n91723\tGroth16/KZG decider over its selected curves and fixes a 128-entry address-book\n91724\tcircuit bound; there is no browser/TypeScript verifier in the inspected source.\n91725\tHandoff refuses a mismatched or incomplete construction, requires the\n91726\tHistory proof metadata to match the Hints verification key for joint promotion,\n91727\tpromotes the completed next construction, purges obsolete votes/messages, and\n91728\tremoves departed-node proof keys where appropriate. Reconnect stops old\n91729\tcontrollers before learned state is installed. Thus safe purge follows a\n91730\tcompleted matching handoff; it is not permission to discard an unadopted source\n91731\tor availability/rollback material prematurely. The assumptions include an\n91732\tauthentic genesis ledger ID, the recursive verifier key and proving artifacts,\n91733\tsound native cryptography, threshold-honest source authorization, sufficient\n91734\ttarget participation for liveness, and available state/block distribution.\n91735\t\n91736\tThe D.110c-0b candidate decision is:\n91737\t\n91738\t| Candidate                                                   | Genesis authentication and evolving authority                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | Epoch growth / costs                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Browser, dependency, and contract impact                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Restart, replay, archive, and pruning disposition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n91739\t| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n91740\t| Stable genesis/root authority signs the current checkpoint  | Directly authenticates a current anchor under the creator key committed by a separately pin-verified genesis trust record. Supports evolving ACL/state/history fields while the `creator-trusted-v1` signer remains invariant; does not solve future seal-key rotation. | O(1) existing-v1 genesis/current record bytes and fixed rollback generations, plus the existing RFC 9162 accumulator's absolute safe-integer ceiling of at most 53 peaks; existing Ed25519 plus anchor/QC/history checks. | Browser-feasible with existing primitives, record schema, and no new crypto dependency or wire field. It still requires a new reviewed protocol-v3 checkpoint opener, closure-law contract, durable compatibility transition, and authenticated freshness floor. | Untrusted genesis/current bytes are fully reverified and cross-bound. A caller-held authenticated expected head/floor is required to reject a valid stale checkpoint; a new client with only genesis cannot infer latestness. The bounded immediate-predecessor rollback closure supplies the genuine epoch-N-1 trust needed to verify the retained N-1→N QC. Archive bytes grow separately. Prune only after authentic durable replacement, head installation, snapshot/availability/outbox gates, and two rollback generations. **Selected for creator-only integrity/control compaction, conditional on D.110c-0b0 selecting a real freshness-floor owner.** |\n91741\t| Periodically certified checkpoint with external pin         | Can authenticate current authority and freshness if the external pin is trusted.                                                                                                                                                                                        | O(1) local control state; external service/state evolves.                                                                                                                                                                 | Introduces a new authority/availability carrier, API, migration, and operational dependency.                                                                                                                                                                     | Useful only as a separately accepted Phase-7 bootstrap/freshness design. **Rejected for D.110c-0b.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n91742\t| WRAPS-like recursively accumulated transition proof         | Authenticates arbitrary changing authority from genesis when each transition is encoded and authorized.                                                                                                                                                                 | Constant-size public proof and fixed verification; nontrivial recursive proving/update work plus retained prior uncompressed proof and proving artifacts.                                                                 | New recursive-SNARK/native dependency, verifier/prover keys or setup artifacts, wire fields, browser cost, compatibility, and migration.                                                                                                                         | Strong compact lineage, but freshness/availability remain separate and operational state is not zero. **Rejected as disproportionate for per-room creator-only scope; any future rotated-authority use is a separate high-risk design.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n91743\t| Merkle/skip consistency proof                               | Proves inclusion/append consistency, not authority transition without separately certified keys.                                                                                                                                                                        | O(log N) proof/state or an equivalent retained accumulator/index.                                                                                                                                                         | Hash-only and browser-friendly, but new schema and control-proof semantics.                                                                                                                                                                                      | RFC 9162 already helps history consistency; this does not independently authenticate changing authority and fails the strict bounded-control target if its required path grows. **Rejected as the authority solution.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n91744\t| Existing anchor + cut/QC + RFC 9162 history root, unchanged | Existing bytes authenticate the first creator transition and append-only history under current assumptions.                                                                                                                                                             | Current closure retains every old cut/QC and therefore grows O(N); opener is epoch-0/first-successor-specific.                                                                                                            | No new dependency, but the unchanged form cannot meet the acceptance contract.                                                                                                                                                                                   | Useful primitives for the selected checkpoint, not a complete age-independent opener. **Rejected unchanged.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n91745\t\n91746\tThe selected D.110c-0b architecture is therefore a **bounded dual-anchor creator\n91747\tcheckpoint with a fixed rollback window**, not Hiero/WRAPS. It reuses the\n91748\texisting v1 trust-record bytes: one immutable genesis record opened against the\n91749\tpinned genesis digest, one current record, the current cut/QC and exact recovery\n91750\tdescriptor, and exactly two complete rollback generations. The opener first\n91751\tauthenticates the genesis record and extracts its creator/profile/signer-set\n91752\tmaterial; it then requires byte-identical profile/signer-set carriers and\n91753\tdigests in the current record, verifies the current anchor and creator\n91754\tsignature, and verifies current cut/QC, ACL, history root and size,\n91755\tarchive-index root, state/snapshot/manifest, and recovery bindings. A\n91756\tself-contained hash or self-signature cannot nominate its own trust key or\n91757\tclaim to be latest.\n91758\t\n91759\tThe logical retention law is exact even though implementation ref names remain\n91760\towned by their existing stores. At epoch N the active generation retains one\n91761\tpin-verifiable genesis trust record, one current trust record, one current live\n91762\tprojection, the current snapshot/manifest/ACL and recovery refs required by the\n91763\texisting reopen owner, and only the cut/QC for transition N-1→N. It retains no\n91764\tearlier transition cut/QC. Exactly two complete prior closures remain in\n91765\t`Superseded` rollback generations under D.109; no third rollback closure, copied\n91766\tjournal chain, bootstrap chain, or sibling registration is permitted. Archive\n91767\tsegments and application history are separately growing content-addressed data\n91768\tand are not required to authenticate the current creator key. The expected-head\n91769\tinput is an authenticated `(objectId, epoch, currentAnchorDigest)` supplied\n91770\toutside the untrusted checkpoint bytes; absence or mismatch is a fail-closed\n91771\tfreshness result, not permission to choose the largest stored epoch.\n91772\t\n91773\tFor epoch N>0, the newest bounded `Superseded` rollback closure must retain and\n91774\treopen the genuine epoch-N-1 creator trust long enough to verify the retained\n91775\tN-1→N commit QC through the ordinary epoch-relative seal authority. The cold\n91776\topener may not mint an authority directly from a naked key/anchor/epoch tuple or\n91777\tsilently downgrade the QC to unverified recovery metadata. This uses one of the\n91778\talready-required two bounded rollback generations; it does not retain an O(N)\n91779\tpredecessor chain. Genesis has no predecessor QC. Absence, ambiguity, or\n91780\tmismatch of that immediate predecessor closure fails closed before current\n91781\ttrust, activation, or deletion.\n91782\t\n91783\tThe predecessor reopen is an explicit recursion terminator, not a second\n91784\tcurrent-head open. It authenticates the retained epoch-N-1 record from the\n91785\tpinned genesis digest, byte-identical profile/signer-set carriers, creator\n91786\tsignature over its own epoch anchor, and\n91787\t`current.previousAnchor === predecessor.currentAnchorDigest`; it does not need\n91788\tto verify the predecessor's own N-2→N-1 QC. Latest-floor equality applies only\n91789\tto the proposed current room head. The immediate predecessor therefore must\n91790\tnot equal the epoch-N latest floor, and no recursive predecessor walk is\n91791\tpermitted beyond the fixed rollback window.\n91792\t\n91793\tThis selection has a hard compatibility boundary. Current protocol-v3 exposes a\n91794\tgenesis-only trust opener, `inspectTrustClosure` permits exactly one trust\n91795\trecord, and the closure law accumulates old cut/QC proof refs. A new or extended\n91796\tauthenticated opener/closure contract and atomic bounded-closure transition are\n91797\ttherefore **D.110c-0b1**, a named high-risk prerequisite; it must prefer a\n91798\tprivate/package-internal composition and preserve the v1 record/wire schema.\n91799\tThe existing exported `inspectTrustClosure`, `assertTrustPreserved`, and install\n91800\tpaths keep their exactly-one-trust-record contract. The first 0b1 stop-check is\n91801\twhether the Node owner can validate two actual closure members through a scoped\n91802\tprivate dual-record inspector without globally relaxing those exports. If the\n91803\tRED demonstrates that a changed exported predicate/result, new public API,\n91804\trecord version, wire field, authority carrier, dependency, or migration\n91805\tprotocol is genuinely required, 0b1 stops and is resliced again rather than\n91806\timplementing it. D.110c-0b1 is\n91807\tlimited to stable `creator-trusted-v1`; rotating creator/seal authority,\n91808\tdelegated/BFT repeated rollover, external pins, and recursive proofs are\n91809\texcluded. Brand-new-client freshness is not created by a root signature: a\n91810\tvalid earlier creator checkpoint is authentic but may be stale. Reopen must\n91811\tcompare against a caller-held authenticated expected head/floor. That room\n91812\ttuple is distinct from storage's AHE `PresentHead`/`ExpectedHead` revision and\n91813\tclosure-digest tuple and may not be derived from the same hostile generation\n91814\tbeing opened. The bounded source audit has now completed that stop-check: no\n91815\texisting production owner supplies this tuple across restart. Every\n91816\t`expectedHead` under `packages/node/src` is an AHE storage head, and the only\n91817\tgenesis-side caller input is the pinned genesis anchor. A valid older\n91818\tcreator-signed checkpoint is therefore authentic but indistinguishable from\n91819\tthe latest checkpoint after complete hostile rollback. **D.110c-0b0** now owns\n91820\tthe required freshness-floor decision and implementation prerequisite; 0b1 may\n91821\tnot begin until 0b0 is reviewed and GREEN. Phase 7 must\n91822\tname how a new client obtains that input or reslice a freshness/availability\n91823\tauthority. Until then, D.110c may claim bounded authentic recovery with an\n91824\tauthenticated expected head, not discovery of latestness from hostile storage\n91825\tusing genesis alone.\n91826\t\n91827\tD.110c-0b1 executable RED is deliberately sequenced **after D.110c-0b0,\n91828\tD.110c-0a, D.110c-a, and D.110c-b GREEN and before D.110c-c RED**. It consumes their real\n91829\tepoch-relative seal, repeat-close, and hot-adoption path to create at least\n91830\tthree genuine sequential creator transitions; the plan-level 0b design decision\n91831\tdoes not pretend those transitions exist earlier. The RED must prove the\n91832\tcurrent epoch>0 trust cannot open without replaying retained predecessors, exact\n91833\tactive closure grows with each cut/QC, a stale but valid earlier checkpoint is\n91834\trejected when a newer authenticated expected floor exists, and no tests-only\n91835\ttrust record or private fixture shortcut can satisfy the path. GREEN must prove:\n91836\t\n91837\t- the pinned genesis digest, creator key/profile/signer set, current epoch,\n91838\t  current anchor signature, cut/QC, ACL digest, state/snapshot/manifest,\n91839\t  history root/size, archive root, and recovery facts are all authenticated;\n91840\t- tampered genesis, current anchor, signature, epoch, predecessor, ACL, cut/QC,\n91841\t  history, archive, state, manifest, recovery reference, or authenticated expected head/floor fails\n91842\t  closed with exact errors before activation or deletion;\n91843\t- replay, stale checkpoint, skipped epoch, substituted checkpoint, same-anchor\n91844\t  double close, head regression, incomplete write, crash between checkpoint and\n91845\t  head CAS, and unavailable replacement cannot advance trust. Same-epoch\n91846\t  creator equivocation is rejected when two records are present or the\n91847\t  authenticated floor selects the other digest; a lone creator-signed fork\n91848\t  without a floor is explicitly not claimed detectable;\n91849\t- only after the new checkpoint and head are durably/authentically installed do\n91850\t  old control proofs outside two complete rollback generations become eligible\n91851\t  for deletion, with availability, snapshot, outbox, and rollback predicates\n91852\t  still authoritative;\n91853\t- active control closure, bootstrap/checkpoint metadata, registrations, AHE\n91854\t  generation-journal rows/blobs, and all stores required for ordinary reopen\n91855\t  remain constant-bounded with transition count. The RFC 9162 accumulator is\n91856\t  separately bounded by its safe-integer maximum of 53 peaks; growing\n91857\t  archive/application bytes are separately enumerated and are not required as\n91858\t  active authority proof; and\n91859\t- legacy epoch-0/first-successor data either migrates deterministically or is\n91860\t  rejected by an explicit compatibility error; no silent reinterpretation is\n91861\t  permitted.\n91862\t\n91863\tIts focused and retained gates include creator seal/close/adoption/reopen,\n91864\tprotocol-v3 trust vectors, snapshot substitution, D.109 cleanup/rollback/\n91865\tavailability/outbox suites, Node and browser storage migrations, crash-point\n91866\treopen, exact closure and store censuses, and adversarial untrusted-storage\n91867\tmutants. D.110c-c consumes the accepted opener and pruning law; D.110c-d proves\n91868\tthe bound through at least 100 genuine transitions. Phase 7 consumes the\n91869\tmulti-epoch producer and separately owns archive-root evolution, archived-byte\n91870\tavailability, cold paging, and brand-new-client freshness. No D.110c-0b result\n91871\tmay hide O(N) authority data in archive, metadata, registration, bootstrap, or\n91872\trecovery storage.\n91873\t\n91874\t##### Explicit implementation reslice\n91875\t\n91876\tThe umbrella closes through named decision and implementation owners. Numeric\n91877\tlabels preserve audit identity; they are not execution order. The exact causal\n91878\torder is: current 0b architecture decision; D.110c-0b0 design acceptance;\n91879\tD.110c-0a GREEN; D.110c-0b0 RED/GREEN; D.110c-a GREEN; D.110c-b GREEN;\n91880\texecutable D.110c-0b1 RED/GREEN; D.110c-0c; D.110c-c; then D.110c-d. The hot\n91881\t0a/a/b path may retain its small, genuinely produced linear\n91882\tclosure only long enough to expose 0b1's causal RED; it cannot claim bounded\n91883\treopen or proceed to cleanup/long-horizon acceptance. No sub-slice may\n91884\tmanufacture an epoch-N capability, anchor, projection, history snapshot, QC,\n91885\treceipt, archive record, or active registration in test code.\n91886\t\n91887\t1. **D.110c-0a — epoch-relative creator seal custody.** Owner:\n91888\t   `packages/protocol-v3/src/seal.ts`, the creator-only owners in\n91889\t   `packages/seal`, the Node vote/evidence ports, and the browser adapters in\n91890\t   `packages/storage-browser/src/seal-evidence.ts`,\n91891\t   `packages/storage-browser/src/internal/seal-vote-store.ts`,\n91892\t   `seal-evidence-store.ts`, and `schema-idb.ts`. RED opens seal\n91893\t   authority and a genuine actor with authenticated epoch-1 creator trust and\n91894\t   proves today's exact epoch-0 refusal without synthesizing a QC. GREEN derives\n91895\t   every proposal/vote/QC/evidence epoch and anchor from opaque trust authority,\n91896\t   isolates write-once evidence/vote custody per authenticated\n91897\t   `(objectId, closedEpoch)`, rejects cross-epoch store reuse, and leaves the\n91898\t   certified/BFT branch plus epoch-0 vectors byte-identical. Its compatibility\n91899\t   boundary explicitly covers existing epoch-0 rows. The current schema-v3 key\n91900\t   paths already include epoch, so no IndexedDB version bump is expected; a\n91901\t   demonstrated schema-version change stops and reslices. The exported\n91902\t   `servePeerEvidence({objectId, signerId})` selector cannot distinguish two\n91903\t   retained epochs even though the existing network request already carries\n91904\t   epoch. The bounded 0a plan must explicitly review a backward-compatible\n91905\t   epoch selector; it may not silently choose the latest row, and any required\n91906\t   public-contract change is declared before 0a RED/GREEN. Its\n91907\t   reclamation law covers `sealEvidence`, `voteSlots`, `signerState`, and\n91908\t   `voteOutbox` only after authenticated handoff plus the existing rollback,\n91909\t   availability, and outbox gates. Those four stores enter the D.110c-c/d\n91910\t   bounded durable census. This is a high-risk security-authority slice and\n91911\t   closes before D.110c-a RED.\n91912\t2. **D.110c-0b0 — authenticated freshness-floor authority prerequisite.**\n91913\t   Owner: protocol-v3 trust opening, the creator signing/seal custody boundary,\n91914\t   Node cold reopen and product-room ownership, with storage, keychain,\n91915\t   availability, and Phase-7 bootstrap as candidate boundaries rather than\n91916\t   assumed authorities. The completed source audit proves there is no current\n91917\t   production-owned `(objectId, epoch, currentAnchorDigest)` floor that survives\n91918\t   restart independently of the untrusted generation being opened. A\n91919\t   creator-signed checkpoint authenticates content but cannot prove latestness\n91920\t   after complete rollback. The design checkpoint must compare at least a\n91921\t   monotonic creator-signing-custody floor, an application/account-held room\n91922\t   pin, and an external checkpoint/availability authority, and must state the\n91923\t   exact trust root, durable/atomic update law, crash behavior, rollback and\n91924\t   recovery law, migration, browser feasibility, public/API/schema impact, and\n91925\t   brand-new-client boundary, per-room O(1) growth law, and authenticated\n91926\t   rollback-safe retirement law. The selected floor owner must be named in the\n91927\t   D.110c-c/d durable censuses. No candidate is silently selected by this audit.\n91928\t   RED uses one genuine 0→1 transition, then presents a complete valid older\n91929\t   room generation from hostile storage and proves current production has no\n91930\t   authenticated input with which to reject it. GREEN must supply one reviewed\n91931\t   production owner and require exact equality between its authenticated room\n91932\t   floor and only the proposed current room head\n91933\t   `(objectId, epoch, currentAnchorDigest)`, rather than maximum-epoch\n91934\t   selection. Every genuine authenticated room-head installation must advance\n91935\t   that floor atomically with the durable head; the floor may advance only with\n91936\t   that head. A floor behind the authenticated durable room head, valid stale\n91937\t   or forked checkpoint, or floor rollback fails closed before activation or\n91938\t   deletion and may never authorize a generation merely because that\n91939\t   generation equals the lagging floor. The retained immediate predecessor is\n91940\t   lineage-authenticated under the separate bounded rule above and does not\n91941\t   match the latest floor. GREEN must recover safely across every crash edge. A\n91942\t   floor stored only beside the hostile checkpoint is invalid. Brand-new-client freshness remains a\n91943\t   separately reviewed Phase-7 authority. If the selected construction needs a\n91944\t   public API, new durable store/field, keychain authority, external service,\n91945\t   migration, dependency, or changed genesis assumption, 0b0 records that\n91946\t   exact high-risk implementation boundary and receives Grok/Kimi/Opus review\n91947\t   before production edits. Its design deadline is before 0a production edits;\n91948\t   its GREEN deadline is before 0b1 RED.\n91949\t3. **D.110c-0b — bounded authenticated trust checkpoint and control-proof\n91950\t   compaction decision.** Owner: protocol-v3/control-plane trust and closure\n91951\t   law, Node creator close, hot adoption verify/commit, cold reopen, and existing\n91952\t   durable owners as boundaries. The design\n91953\t   must permit age-independent cold reopen from the pinned genesis while\n91954\t   bounding the active control closure and without trusting storage bytes,\n91955\t   retaining an O(N) proof chain, weakening the genesis pin, or moving growth\n91956\t   into an uncounted store. Current trust records, live-journal anchor carriers,\n91957\t   and retained cut/QC evidence are inputs to the audit. The comparative audit\n91958\t   selects the existing-v1 dual-anchor creator checkpoint plus two rollback\n91959\t   generations and rejects WRAPS, an external pin, and a Merkle proof as the\n91960\t   authority solution, conditional on D.110c-0b0 supplying the missing floor.\n91961\t   D.110c-0b1 owns its reviewed opener/closure contract, exact\n91962\t   authenticated-head input, compatibility transition, RED/GREEN, and\n91963\t   stop boundary; it adds no new record/wire schema unless another high-risk\n91964\t   reslice is first accepted. Its executable RED waits for 0b0/0a/a/b GREEN and\n91965\t   consumes their real transition loop; it closes before D.110c-c RED and\n91966\t   D.110c-d freeze.\n91967\t4. **D.110c-0c — durable pending-adoption resume decision.** Owner: creator\n91968\t   close/adoption durable orchestration and existing AHE/snapshot/evidence\n91969\t   owners. RED must create a real closed durable head, terminate the process\n91970\t   before adoption, and prove no genuine reopen/resume entry exists. GREEN, if\n91971\t   separately approved, reconstructs only authenticated close/adoption facts\n91972\t   and resumes the existing verify/CAS/activate path; it never treats a pending\n91973\t   head as adopted. A new public entry or authority carrier is explicitly\n91974\t   reviewed, not smuggled into D.110c-c. It closes before the post-close and\n91975\t   pre-adoption restart gates and executes only after D.110c-0b1 GREEN.\n91976\t5. **D.110c-a — authenticated repeat-close state carrier.** Owner:\n91977\t   `packages/node/src/creator-close.ts` and the private installed-v3 close\n91978\t   registration in `v3-live.ts`, with compaction as unchanged verifier and 0a\n91979\t   as prerequisite. RED binds a real adopted epoch-1 plane and calls the genuine\n91980\t   close path; after isolating the 0a seal refusal, it fails because the exact\n91981\t   prior accumulator is unavailable/mismatched. GREEN creates one authenticated\n91982\t   epoch-continuity owner in prepared/live registration state, supplies it to\n91983\t   existing history derivation, and generalizes the exported close-result epoch\n91984\t   fields under a separately pinned public contract. Root/size mismatch, reset,\n91985\t   substitution, same-anchor double close, stale handle, and duplicate-close\n91986\t   mutants fail closed. This slice owns the epoch-N snapshot-substitution proof:\n91987\t   a snapshot or manifest from any earlier epoch/anchor must fail before state,\n91988\t   ACL, or recovery ownership changes. Every generalized genuine close that\n91989\t   durably installs a new authenticated room head must invoke the accepted 0b0\n91990\t   atomic floor-advance law; omitting or lagging that advance fails closed.\n91991\t   Deadline: before D.110c-b RED.\n91992\t6. **D.110c-b — general hot adoption, activation, and product custody.** Owner:\n91993\t   creator adoption/commit/activate modules, `creator-successor-live`,\n91994\t   installed-v3 handoff, and `examples/v3-room`. RED executes real 0→1 and\n91995\t   attempts 1→2; it pins first-transition projection constants, the topic-keyed\n91996\t   stale-wrapper success, missing successor close rebinding, and the room's\n91997\t   one-transition adoption latch. GREEN uses authenticated epochs/kinds,\n91998\t   replaces active ownership only for the exact next anchor, rebinds close after\n91999\t   every adoption, and permits the room to request the next genuine adoption.\n92000\t   Every generalized adoption/activation must observe the accepted 0b0 room\n92001\t   floor at the newly installed head; it cannot activate against a missing,\n92002\t   lagging, or independently advanced floor.\n92003\t   Existing first-transition APIs and epoch-1 governance controls remain valid;\n92004\t   new epoch-N controls are additive. Deadline: before D.110c-c RED.\n92005\t7. **D.110c-c — bounded cold reopen, repeated cleanup, restart, and custody.**\n92006\t   Owner: the existing D.109 cleanup/reclamation orchestrators and installed-v3\n92007\t   registration custody, consuming approved 0b/0c designs. RED/Green cover\n92008\t   fresh-process pre-close, post-close, pre-adoption, post-adoption, and\n92009\t   post-prune boundaries; age-independent trust reopen; offline/rebase outbox;\n92010\t   sequential prune plus one skipped-prune catch-up; two rollback generations;\n92011\t   availability refusal; receipt replay/staleness; active-owner uniqueness;\n92012\t   predecessor terminalization; bounded hot/control closure; and bounded live\n92013\t   registration/close ownership through at least three genuine transitions. Its\n92014\t   exact durable census includes browser `sealEvidence`, `voteSlots`,\n92015\t   `signerState`, and `voteOutbox` rows by epoch plus the selected 0b0 freshness\n92016\t   floor owner, and proves their authenticated, rollback-safe retirement rather\n92017\t   than merely counting AHE generations.\n92018\t   Physical store deletion authority remains unchanged unless a separate RED\n92019\t   demonstrates an owner defect. Deadline: before D.110c-d RED.\n92020\t8. **D.110c-d — retained long-horizon and golden-path prerequisite gate.**\n92021\t   Owner: tests and test/build infrastructure only after D.110c-a/b/c GREEN. One\n92022\t   room performs at least 100 genuine authenticated transitions. The exact bound\n92023\t   remains 100 unless this high-risk plan review approves another explicit value\n92024\t   before RED. It runs no adaptive retry and cannot replace real close/adopt\n92025\t   calls with synthetic maintenance. The retained durable census includes all\n92026\t   four creator-seal browser stores, the selected 0b0 freshness-floor owner,\n92027\t   and the Node control owners. Deadline:\n92028\t   before Phase-6 exit or Phase-7a execution.\n92029\t\n92030\t##### D.110c-0a bounded epoch-relative seal-custody plan\n92031\t\n92032\tThe completed source audit pins the exact defect and prevents a broader seal\n92033\tredesign. `packages/protocol-v3/src/seal.ts` holds `SealAuthorityState.epoch` as\n92034\tliteral `0`, rejects genuine creator trust when `material.currentEpoch !== 0`,\n92035\tand registers every authority identity at epoch 0. `packages/seal` repeats that\n92036\tliteral in voter enrollment, vote/round-change intent, pacemaker event, and\n92037\tcreator-close evidence types. The browser `voteSlots`, `signerState`,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b68b30f-133f-4078-a9a7-ee7aeeec8953","uuid":"d6fdd062-cad6-4dcd-874d-f95ed4ba821a","timestamp":"2026-09-02T08:25:24.018Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/aristotle/Documents/Projects/ts-drp-1/docs/production-hardening/production-hardening-tdd-plan-v2.md","content":"#### D.110c — blocking repeated same-room authenticated epoch lifecycle\n\n##### Inherited evidence, boundary, owner, and deadline\n\nD.110c preserves D.108, D.109, and D.110a exactly as accepted. D.108 proves the\nfirst genuine creator transition from epoch 0 to epoch 1. D.109f proves a\n128-step synthetic durable-maintenance differential plus one genuine lifecycle;\nit explicitly does not claim repeated installed-v3 rollover. D.110a proves\ndistinct-room churn, per-handoff reclamation, exact million-operation accounting,\nand bounded retained memory across 64 rooms that each close only once. None is\nreopened, rerun, weakened, or relabelled as repeated epochs of one room.\n\nD.110c is a blocking coordinated production-lifecycle umbrella owned by the\n`@ts-drp/node` installed-v3 creator close/adoption/reopen orchestrators, with the\nexisting `@ts-drp/protocol-v3`, compaction, storage, issuance, snapshot,\navailability, archive, and runtime-reclamation owners retained as verification\nboundaries. Its deadline is before Phase-6 exit, before D.110b or any later gate\nis used to claim bounded memory _after E epochs_, and before Phase 7a/7b or either\ngolden path consumes a long-lived-room claim. D.110b's CI trend wiring may not\nerase or satisfy this blocker.\n\nThis checkpoint is audit and plan only. It authorizes no production edit, RED,\ncampaign, wire-format change, authority change, dependency change, threshold\nchange, public API, or Phase-7 implementation. The bounded source audit below\ndemonstrates that the gap is not merely test composition or one private Node\norchestration bug. It crosses an exported result contract, creator seal\nauthority/evidence custody, bounded authenticated trust recovery, product-room\nownership, and durable transition recovery. Those owners are explicitly\nresliced before any production change.\n\n##### Bounded source and architecture audit\n\nSome close primitives are epoch-relative, but the complete production path is\nnot. `prepareCreatorClose` derives the cut epoch from authenticated\n`currentTrust`, enforces exact history extension, and binds the successor\nanchor. By contrast, creator seal custody and several Node/product owners are\nstill epoch-0/first-successor-only:\n\n| Lifecycle concern                                        | Current production path                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | Exact repeated-epoch gap and required custody                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Live issue and publication                               | `v3-live.ts` activates the adopted successor as an ordinary non-`snapshot-closed` plane; `issueLocal`, pending/retained publication, ingress, and stable topic use the recovered epoch and anchor.                                                                                                                                                                                            | The live path already works at epoch 1. Preserve it and prove the first post-epoch-2 issue, publication, ingress, journal row, reducer state, and digest rather than adding another issuing API.                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Close set and compact history                            | `creatorCloseRegistration` captures the genuine graph and authenticated anchor; `creator-close.ts` calls `deriveCloseSetHistoryCommitment`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | The close owner always supplies a new empty accumulator. Epoch 1 commits nonzero prior history, so the genuine second close fails exact accumulator/root/size verification. The adopted projection retains `compactHistory`, but the live registration neither authenticates nor carries one shared continuity owner.                                                                           |\n| Creator seal and QC authority                            | `openSealAuthority` in protocol-v3 refuses any creator trust whose `currentEpoch !== 0`; protocol seal authority, proposal, vote, and QC identities bind literal epoch 0. `@ts-drp/seal` creator intent, vote intent, pacemaker, evidence, vote stores, and browser adapters also carry epoch-0 literals, and the singleton evidence actor cannot reuse an epoch-0 finalized row for epoch 1. | No genuine epoch-1 cut can be certified or verified today. Generalizing creator-only seal custody and selecting evidence/vote custody by authenticated `(objectId, closedEpoch)` is a security-authority and durable-record contract, not a Node-only fix. Certified/BFT epoch-zero behavior and all existing vectors remain unchanged.                                                         |\n| Exported close result                                    | `@ts-drp/node/creator-close` publicly exports `CreatorLiveCloseResult` with literal `epoch: 0` and `successorEpoch: 1`; the runtime returns the same constants.                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Repeated close requires one reviewed public-contract generalization to safe integer epochs with exact `successorEpoch === epoch + 1`. Field names and result shape remain unchanged; this is still a public API change and is never hidden as private plumbing.                                                                                                                                 |\n| Snapshot and manifest                                    | Snapshot staging/export/quarantine bind the registration's current object, epoch, anchor, ACL, and state digest.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | The existing format appears epoch-relative. Prove epoch-N export and epoch-N+1 verification cannot substitute an earlier snapshot; stop if a format change is demonstrated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| Next anchor, projection, and adoption                    | `creator-adoption.ts` verifies the first history advance and emits `v3-live-generation-2`, while hot verify/commit code requires predecessor kind `v3-live-generation-1` and constructs epoch 1.                                                                                                                                                                                              | Generalize existing epoch fields and choose predecessor kind from authenticated epoch. Do not add a new projection kind or fixture-authored capability.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| Activation and product custody                           | Adoption uses one-use capabilities and strict CAS. `creator-adoption-activate.ts` keys `activeOwners` only by stable topic and returns the existing wrapper when bindings match. `examples/v3-room` binds close only for genesis, treats a set successor authority as permanently adopted, and does not bind close after cold reopen.                                                         | A second adoption can report success while returning the stale epoch-1 wrapper. The product owner must replace ownership only for the exact next authenticated anchor, rebind close to every adopted/reopened successor, and preserve the stale-handle refusal contract.                                                                                                                        |\n| Cold reopen and trust root                               | Reopen assumes the first three-generation shape, genesis projection, one cut/QC, epoch-1 successor, and the genesis-only trust opener. `inspectTrustClosure` permits exactly one trust record and every trust advance removes the prior record.                                                                                                                                               | After repeated D.109 pruning, no bounded authenticated carrier currently proves an arbitrary current trust from the pinned genesis. Replaying an ever-growing cut/QC chain or retaining every old trust record would violate age-independent recovery or bounded durable-control state. A reviewed trust-checkpoint/control-proof compaction design is required before implementation.          |\n| Durable control-plane census                             | `exactCombinedClosure` retains every old cut and commit-QC ref while replacing only the trust record; adoption also retains predecessor ACL material.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | The current active closure grows linearly with epochs. The user's bounded durable-structure gate may not be weakened to accept exact linear `f(N)`. The checkpoint design must bound hot/control closure and recovery state while accounting archive/history data separately. Any change to closure law, trust opening, or authenticated checkpoint bytes is high-risk and separately reviewed. |\n| Closed-but-unadopted restart                             | Verified adoption facts are attached to the in-process close handle; no genuine cross-process entry reconstructs and resumes a durable closed/pending-adoption state.                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | The required post-close and pre-adoption restart gates need a named durable resume capability. If it requires a new public API or authority carrier, that change is its own reviewed prerequisite; the gate is not dropped or relabelled as in-process recovery.                                                                                                                                |\n| Offline/rebase outbox                                    | Successor activation carries the issuance store/scope and live journal, reconstructs displaced-source authority, and exposes rebase completion.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Prove no pending/published row is lost, duplicated, reassigned, or accepted under a stale epoch; preserve the issuance watermark and first valid post-reopen publication.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| Rollback, availability, pruning, and runtime reclamation | D.109 planning is epoch-numeric and deletes only after verified adoption, exact head, two rollback generations, policy, snapshot, and categorized outbox. D.109d binds `closedEpoch` to successor `closedEpoch + 1`.                                                                                                                                                                          | Reuse these owners only with authentic per-transition facts. Prove sequential prune/catch-up, distinct receipts, idempotent replay, two rollback generations, and bounded live predecessor/registration custody. A failure may retain too much but never delete too much.                                                                                                                       |\n| Archive and cold paging                                  | The current anchor/cut schemas carry `archiveIndexRoot`, but protocol-v3 creator close requires the next input root to equal the current anchor root. Phase 7 archive segmentation/paging is not implemented in the production packages.                                                                                                                                                      | D.110c can supply a genuine multi-epoch producer and preserve an existing root, but cannot silently evolve archive lineage or cold-page messages. Evolving the committed archive root is a separate Phase-7 wire/authority reslice. The Discord golden path stays blocked until that downstream slice consumes D.110c output.                                                                   |\n\nThe genuine second close therefore has several ordered failures, not one: an\nepoch-1 seal actor cannot open or certify a QC; the close history owner has only\nan empty prior accumulator; the exported close result and adoption path encode\n0→1; activation can return the stale topic owner; and restart/pruning have no\nbounded authenticated current-trust checkpoint. D.110c is a coordinated\nproduct/security lifecycle program. It already demonstrates a need for a\nbounded public result-contract change and separate security-authority,\ndurable-checkpoint, control-closure, and resume decisions. That is why this\ncheckpoint stops at audit/reslice. Any registry kind/domain, wire field,\ndependency, workload, threshold, availability/deletion rule, or additional\npublic surface beyond the explicitly reviewed owners below stops the affected\nsub-slice for another high-risk reslice.\n\n##### D.110c-0b Hedera/Hiero comparative authority-lineage audit\n\nThis bounded comparison is design evidence, not an implementation dependency or\nauthorization. The audit pinned `hiero-improvement-proposals` at commit\n[`54ccb06659592ab201e7adea632f1019e9faa00e`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/tree/54ccb06659592ab201e7adea632f1019e9faa00e)\n(tree `42ff7d2c1ff68ef662b405cb167838849d1b49f1`) and HIP-1200 blob\n`088088185786375a1478166bbd61c4021eedc85c`; it pinned\n`hiero-consensus-node` at commit\n[`1aa1d6c153907750cfbba6935b7a21867053968e`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/tree/1aa1d6c153907750cfbba6935b7a21867053968e)\n(tree `7c7adb3015c782e4131cc2ad1ca7ec5f97cb8cc5`). The inspected\nexact-weight design blob is `c35cb34e6e797719fdb02f8541cb067f64e3972a`;\nthe implementation blobs for `HistoryLibrary`, `HistoryLibraryImpl`,\n`WrapsHistoryProver`, `WritableHistoryStoreImpl`, `V071HistorySchema`,\n`TssHandoffCoordinator`, `WrapsProvingKeyVerification`, and\n`history_types.proto` are respectively\n`c4c998d06d611fe7ece9c62346fb6fad4c7a671c`,\n`c53deacbffe3352a0dba74d46cc7ddf265f4ed6c`,\n`fc7c893b20b9246be8f2b814af7810500ce4fa2a`,\n`589537503db6da008b876b540c37664cbb9665ba`,\n`0cb24998101688d3fe0f09986cceea780fd213d4`,\n`8ca7f9a87c853663add1bfb194b6017a80c6a2ff`,\n`e15e6c352a085d3d44b3ac433712b6dfcc070647`, and\n`5b4d59d4002426af096423af879390f883eda1ff`. The pinned test blobs for\nhistory-store handoff/purge, WRAPS handoff, reconnect reset, and signed-state\nvalidation are also retained in the audit ledger. The native implementation was\nindependently pinned at `hiero-cryptography` commit\n[`39f28f39f609f80e52253d86169e2db5216a713e`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-cryptography/tree/39f28f39f609f80e52253d86169e2db5216a713e),\nwith WRAPS `lib.rs`/Cargo blobs\n`5beccdf8fdf35a5da6f55112663faa1b829bd849` and\n`f5aba82bedd5d17b8127e211ecf3d97632eb0a5a`. These pins distinguish\nwhat was inspected from later upstream movement.\n\nHIP-1200 is an approved proposal that defines a stable ledger ID as the hash of\nthe genesis TSS address book and a chain-of-trust proof for a current history.\nThe pinned consensus source demonstrates that `HistoryService`, genesis and\nincremental WRAPS construction, compressed-proof verification, persisted\nactive/next constructions, joint Hints/History handoff, reconnect reset, and\nhandoff purging are implemented on that revision. Source and tests do **not** by\nthemselves prove a specific public-network deployment or operational enablement;\nthis audit makes no such claim. In particular, the pinned `TssConfig` still\ndefaults `forceMockSignatures` to true pending a full block-stream cutover. The\nexact-weight document is architecture documentation. HIP-1200 describes packed\nblock-signature sizes and notes that ArkWorks/snarkjs compatibility is\nunconfirmed; the implemented protobuf uses one opaque `block_signature` byte\nfield. Proposal sizes/layout and implementation defaults are recorded\nseparately and are not ts-drp acceptance constants.\n\nThe transferable comparison is deliberately narrow:\n\n| Hiero concept                   | ts-drp analogue                                 | Important non-equivalence                                                                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Genesis roster hash / ledger ID | Pinned room genesis anchor                      | A network-wide roster root is not a per-room creator key, and genesis authenticity remains out-of-band in both systems.                               |\n| Evolving roster and proof keys  | Evolving room ACL/authority                     | In D.110c's `creator-trusted-v1` scope, the creator checkpoint signer/profile/signer set stays fixed while the signed anchor's ACL digest may evolve. |\n| Roster transition and handoff   | Authenticated epoch close, adoption, activation | Hiero coordinates two services and a roster; ts-drp coordinates cut/QC, snapshot, trust, product ownership, and cleanup.                              |\n| Threshold signature             | Cut/QC certification                            | Hiero uses exact weights; creator-only ts-drp has one trusted signer and must not be relabelled BFT.                                                  |\n| Recursive WRAPS proof           | Candidate compact authority-lineage proof       | It is a design family only; no WRAPS, SNARK, BLS/hinTS, CRS, or proving artifact enters ts-drp implicitly.                                            |\n| Current signed state/history    | Authenticated room trust/checkpoint state       | A valid signed checkpoint establishes authenticity, not freshness against withholding or replay by itself.                                            |\n| Block/mirror history            | Archived room epochs/messages                   | Historical application bytes are separately growing archive state, not active authority/control proof.                                                |\n\nThe pinned Hiero construction establishes the following and no more. A verifier\nauthenticates the ledger ID out of band and verifies a compressed recursive\nproof whose initial state binds the genesis address-book hash and whose final\nstate binds the Hints verification-key hash. The inspected compressed verifier\ndoes **not** separately bind the final roster hash, transition count, or\nfreshness. Current-roster selection and freshness remain obligations of the\nsurrounding signed-state/block-number and caller-held-floor owners, not\nproperties inherited from WRAPS. Genesis starts with aggregate Schnorr signatures;\neach incremental construction witnesses the prior uncompressed recursive proof,\nthe source address book/proof keys, the target address book and verification\nkey, and source-roster authorization of that transition. The Rust recursive\ncircuit enforces strictly more than one-half of the preceding roster's weight\nfor the aggregate Schnorr authorization. The separate off-circuit\nrecursive-proof vote-acceptance threshold is at least one-third of source\nweight, while target key readiness is strictly greater than two-thirds.\nHIP-1200 itself uses both the greater-than-one-half and one-third descriptions\nin different sections. Those distinct proof, vote-acceptance, and liveness\nthresholds must not be collapsed into one claim or imported into ts-drp's\nquorum law. Hiero reconnect freshness is likewise external to WRAPS:\n`DefaultSignedStateValidator.throwIfOld` compares learned state with the\nlearner's caller-held prior round/timestamp floor.\n\nThe externally presented recursive proof and its verification cost are\nage-independent, but the complete system is not stateless. It retains the\nledger ID, an active construction, at most one next construction, current proof\nkeys, construction-scoped votes/messages while in flight, the target history,\nand an uncompressed WRAPS proof needed to extend recursion. The prover also\nrequires four separately installed, hash-verified proving/verification artifact\nfiles. The pinned native Rust/JNI implementation uses Nova folding with a\nGroth16/KZG decider over its selected curves and fixes a 128-entry address-book\ncircuit bound; there is no browser/TypeScript verifier in the inspected source.\nHandoff refuses a mismatched or incomplete construction, requires the\nHistory proof metadata to match the Hints verification key for joint promotion,\npromotes the completed next construction, purges obsolete votes/messages, and\nremoves departed-node proof keys where appropriate. Reconnect stops old\ncontrollers before learned state is installed. Thus safe purge follows a\ncompleted matching handoff; it is not permission to discard an unadopted source\nor availability/rollback material prematurely. The assumptions include an\nauthentic genesis ledger ID, the recursive verifier key and proving artifacts,\nsound native cryptography, threshold-honest source authorization, sufficient\ntarget participation for liveness, and available state/block distribution.\n\nThe D.110c-0b candidate decision is:\n\n| Candidate                                                   | Genesis authentication and evolving authority                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | Epoch growth / costs                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Browser, dependency, and contract impact                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Restart, replay, archive, and pruning disposition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Stable genesis/root authority signs the current checkpoint  | Directly authenticates a current anchor under the creator key committed by a separately pin-verified genesis trust record. Supports evolving ACL/state/history fields while the `creator-trusted-v1` signer remains invariant; does not solve future seal-key rotation. | O(1) existing-v1 genesis/current record bytes and fixed rollback generations, plus the existing RFC 9162 accumulator's absolute safe-integer ceiling of at most 53 peaks; existing Ed25519 plus anchor/QC/history checks. | Browser-feasible with existing primitives, record schema, and no new crypto dependency or wire field. It still requires a new reviewed protocol-v3 checkpoint opener, closure-law contract, durable compatibility transition, and authenticated freshness floor. | Untrusted genesis/current bytes are fully reverified and cross-bound. A caller-held authenticated expected head/floor is required to reject a valid stale checkpoint; a new client with only genesis cannot infer latestness. The bounded immediate-predecessor rollback closure supplies the genuine epoch-N-1 trust needed to verify the retained N-1→N QC. Archive bytes grow separately. Prune only after authentic durable replacement, head installation, snapshot/availability/outbox gates, and two rollback generations. **Selected for creator-only integrity/control compaction, conditional on D.110c-0b0 selecting a real freshness-floor owner.** |\n| Periodically certified checkpoint with external pin         | Can authenticate current authority and freshness if the external pin is trusted.                                                                                                                                                                                        | O(1) local control state; external service/state evolves.                                                                                                                                                                 | Introduces a new authority/availability carrier, API, migration, and operational dependency.                                                                                                                                                                     | Useful only as a separately accepted Phase-7 bootstrap/freshness design. **Rejected for D.110c-0b.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| WRAPS-like recursively accumulated transition proof         | Authenticates arbitrary changing authority from genesis when each transition is encoded and authorized.                                                                                                                                                                 | Constant-size public proof and fixed verification; nontrivial recursive proving/update work plus retained prior uncompressed proof and proving artifacts.                                                                 | New recursive-SNARK/native dependency, verifier/prover keys or setup artifacts, wire fields, browser cost, compatibility, and migration.                                                                                                                         | Strong compact lineage, but freshness/availability remain separate and operational state is not zero. **Rejected as disproportionate for per-room creator-only scope; any future rotated-authority use is a separate high-risk design.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| Merkle/skip consistency proof                               | Proves inclusion/append consistency, not authority transition without separately certified keys.                                                                                                                                                                        | O(log N) proof/state or an equivalent retained accumulator/index.                                                                                                                                                         | Hash-only and browser-friendly, but new schema and control-proof semantics.                                                                                                                                                                                      | RFC 9162 already helps history consistency; this does not independently authenticate changing authority and fails the strict bounded-control target if its required path grows. **Rejected as the authority solution.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| Existing anchor + cut/QC + RFC 9162 history root, unchanged | Existing bytes authenticate the first creator transition and append-only history under current assumptions.                                                                                                                                                             | Current closure retains every old cut/QC and therefore grows O(N); opener is epoch-0/first-successor-specific.                                                                                                            | No new dependency, but the unchanged form cannot meet the acceptance contract.                                                                                                                                                                                   | Useful primitives for the selected checkpoint, not a complete age-independent opener. **Rejected unchanged.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n\nThe selected D.110c-0b architecture is therefore a **bounded dual-anchor creator\ncheckpoint with a fixed rollback window**, not Hiero/WRAPS. It reuses the\nexisting v1 trust-record bytes: one immutable genesis record opened against the\npinned genesis digest, one current record, the current cut/QC and exact recovery\ndescriptor, and exactly two complete rollback generations. The opener first\nauthenticates the genesis record and extracts its creator/profile/signer-set\nmaterial; it then requires byte-identical profile/signer-set carriers and\ndigests in the current record, verifies the current anchor and creator\nsignature, and verifies current cut/QC, ACL, history root and size,\narchive-index root, state/snapshot/manifest, and recovery bindings. A\nself-contained hash or self-signature cannot nominate its own trust key or\nclaim to be latest.\n\nThe logical retention law is exact even though implementation ref names remain\nowned by their existing stores. At epoch N the active generation retains one\npin-verifiable genesis trust record, one current trust record, one current live\nprojection, the current snapshot/manifest/ACL and recovery refs required by the\nexisting reopen owner, and only the cut/QC for transition N-1→N. It retains no\nearlier transition cut/QC. Exactly two complete prior closures remain in\n`Superseded` rollback generations under D.109; no third rollback closure, copied\njournal chain, bootstrap chain, or sibling registration is permitted. Archive\nsegments and application history are separately growing content-addressed data\nand are not required to authenticate the current creator key. The expected-head\ninput is an authenticated `(objectId, epoch, currentAnchorDigest)` supplied\noutside the untrusted checkpoint bytes; absence or mismatch is a fail-closed\nfreshness result, not permission to choose the largest stored epoch.\n\nFor epoch N>0, the newest bounded `Superseded` rollback closure must retain and\nreopen the genuine epoch-N-1 creator trust long enough to verify the retained\nN-1→N commit QC through the ordinary epoch-relative seal authority. The cold\nopener may not mint an authority directly from a naked key/anchor/epoch tuple or\nsilently downgrade the QC to unverified recovery metadata. This uses one of the\nalready-required two bounded rollback generations; it does not retain an O(N)\npredecessor chain. Genesis has no predecessor QC. Absence, ambiguity, or\nmismatch of that immediate predecessor closure fails closed before current\ntrust, activation, or deletion.\n\nThe predecessor reopen is an explicit recursion terminator, not a second\ncurrent-head open. It authenticates the retained epoch-N-1 record from the\npinned genesis digest, byte-identical profile/signer-set carriers, creator\nsignature over its own epoch anchor, and\n`current.previousAnchor === predecessor.currentAnchorDigest`; it does not need\nto verify the predecessor's own N-2→N-1 QC. Latest-floor equality applies only\nto the proposed current room head. The immediate predecessor therefore must\nnot equal the epoch-N latest floor, and no recursive predecessor walk is\npermitted beyond the fixed rollback window.\n\nThis selection has a hard compatibility boundary. Current protocol-v3 exposes a\ngenesis-only trust opener, `inspectTrustClosure` permits exactly one trust\nrecord, and the closure law accumulates old cut/QC proof refs. A new or extended\nauthenticated opener/closure contract and atomic bounded-closure transition are\ntherefore **D.110c-0b1**, a named high-risk prerequisite; it must prefer a\nprivate/package-internal composition and preserve the v1 record/wire schema.\nThe existing exported `inspectTrustClosure`, `assertTrustPreserved`, and install\npaths keep their exactly-one-trust-record contract. The first 0b1 stop-check is\nwhether the Node owner can validate two actual closure members through a scoped\nprivate dual-record inspector without globally relaxing those exports. If the\nRED demonstrates that a changed exported predicate/result, new public API,\nrecord version, wire field, authority carrier, dependency, or migration\nprotocol is genuinely required, 0b1 stops and is resliced again rather than\nimplementing it. D.110c-0b1 is\nlimited to stable `creator-trusted-v1`; rotating creator/seal authority,\ndelegated/BFT repeated rollover, external pins, and recursive proofs are\nexcluded. Brand-new-client freshness is not created by a root signature: a\nvalid earlier creator checkpoint is authentic but may be stale. Reopen must\ncompare against a caller-held authenticated expected head/floor. That room\ntuple is distinct from storage's AHE `PresentHead`/`ExpectedHead` revision and\nclosure-digest tuple and may not be derived from the same hostile generation\nbeing opened. The bounded source audit has now completed that stop-check: no\nexisting production owner supplies this tuple across restart. Every\n`expectedHead` under `packages/node/src` is an AHE storage head, and the only\ngenesis-side caller input is the pinned genesis anchor. A valid older\ncreator-signed checkpoint is therefore authentic but indistinguishable from\nthe latest checkpoint after complete hostile rollback. **D.110c-0b0** now owns\nthe required freshness-floor decision and implementation prerequisite; 0b1 may\nnot begin until 0b0 is reviewed and GREEN. Phase 7 must\nname how a new client obtains that input or reslice a freshness/availability\nauthority. Until then, D.110c may claim bounded authentic recovery with an\nauthenticated expected head, not discovery of latestness from hostile storage\nusing genesis alone.\n\nD.110c-0b1 executable RED is deliberately sequenced **after D.110c-0b0,\nD.110c-0a, D.110c-a, and D.110c-b GREEN and before D.110c-c RED**. It consumes their real\nepoch-relative seal, repeat-close, and hot-adoption path to create at least\nthree genuine sequential creator transitions; the plan-level 0b design decision\ndoes not pretend those transitions exist earlier. The RED must prove the\ncurrent epoch>0 trust cannot open without replaying retained predecessors, exact\nactive closure grows with each cut/QC, a stale but valid earlier checkpoint is\nrejected when a newer authenticated expected floor exists, and no tests-only\ntrust record or private fixture shortcut can satisfy the path. GREEN must prove:\n\n- the pinned genesis digest, creator key/profile/signer set, current epoch,\n  current anchor signature, cut/QC, ACL digest, state/snapshot/manifest,\n  history root/size, archive root, and recovery facts are all authenticated;\n- tampered genesis, current anchor, signature, epoch, predecessor, ACL, cut/QC,\n  history, archive, state, manifest, recovery reference, or authenticated expected head/floor fails\n  closed with exact errors before activation or deletion;\n- replay, stale checkpoint, skipped epoch, substituted checkpoint, same-anchor\n  double close, head regression, incomplete write, crash between checkpoint and\n  head CAS, and unavailable replacement cannot advance trust. Same-epoch\n  creator equivocation is rejected when two records are present or the\n  authenticated floor selects the other digest; a lone creator-signed fork\n  without a floor is explicitly not claimed detectable;\n- only after the new checkpoint and head are durably/authentically installed do\n  old control proofs outside two complete rollback generations become eligible\n  for deletion, with availability, snapshot, outbox, and rollback predicates\n  still authoritative;\n- active control closure, bootstrap/checkpoint metadata, registrations, AHE\n  generation-journal rows/blobs, and all stores required for ordinary reopen\n  remain constant-bounded with transition count. The RFC 9162 accumulator is\n  separately bounded by its safe-integer maximum of 53 peaks; growing\n  archive/application bytes are separately enumerated and are not required as\n  active authority proof; and\n- legacy epoch-0/first-successor data either migrates deterministically or is\n  rejected by an explicit compatibility error; no silent reinterpretation is\n  permitted.\n\nIts focused and retained gates include creator seal/close/adoption/reopen,\nprotocol-v3 trust vectors, snapshot substitution, D.109 cleanup/rollback/\navailability/outbox suites, Node and browser storage migrations, crash-point\nreopen, exact closure and store censuses, and adversarial untrusted-storage\nmutants. D.110c-c consumes the accepted opener and pruning law; D.110c-d proves\nthe bound through at least 100 genuine transitions. Phase 7 consumes the\nmulti-epoch producer and separately owns archive-root evolution, archived-byte\navailability, cold paging, and brand-new-client freshness. No D.110c-0b result\nmay hide O(N) authority data in archive, metadata, registration, bootstrap, or\nrecovery storage.\n\n##### Explicit implementation reslice\n\nThe umbrella closes through named decision and implementation owners. Numeric\nlabels preserve audit identity; they are not execution order. The exact causal\norder is: current 0b architecture decision; D.110c-0b0 design acceptance;\nD.110c-0a GREEN; D.110c-0b0 RED/GREEN; D.110c-a GREEN; D.110c-b GREEN;\nexecutable D.110c-0b1 RED/GREEN; D.110c-0c; D.110c-c; then D.110c-d. The hot\n0a/a/b path may retain its small, genuinely produced linear\nclosure only long enough to expose 0b1's causal RED; it cannot claim bounded\nreopen or proceed to cleanup/long-horizon acceptance. No sub-slice may\nmanufacture an epoch-N capability, anchor, projection, history snapshot, QC,\nreceipt, archive record, or active registration in test code.\n\n1. **D.110c-0a — epoch-relative creator seal custody.** Owner:\n   `packages/protocol-v3/src/seal.ts`, the creator-only owners in\n   `packages/seal`, the Node vote/evidence ports, and the browser adapters in\n   `packages/storage-browser/src/seal-evidence.ts`,\n   `packages/storage-browser/src/internal/seal-vote-store.ts`,\n   `seal-evidence-store.ts`, and `schema-idb.ts`. RED opens seal\n   authority and a genuine actor with authenticated epoch-1 creator trust and\n   proves today's exact epoch-0 refusal without synthesizing a QC. GREEN derives\n   every proposal/vote/QC/evidence epoch and anchor from opaque trust authority,\n   isolates write-once evidence/vote custody per authenticated\n   `(objectId, closedEpoch)`, rejects cross-epoch store reuse, and leaves the\n   certified/BFT branch plus epoch-0 vectors byte-identical. Its compatibility\n   boundary explicitly covers existing epoch-0 rows. The current schema-v3 key\n   paths already include epoch, so no IndexedDB version bump is expected; a\n   demonstrated schema-version change stops and reslices. The exported\n   `servePeerEvidence({objectId, signerId})` selector cannot distinguish two\n   retained epochs even though the existing network request already carries\n   epoch. The bounded 0a plan must explicitly review a backward-compatible\n   epoch selector; it may not silently choose the latest row, and any required\n   public-contract change is declared before 0a RED/GREEN. Its\n   reclamation law covers `sealEvidence`, `voteSlots`, `signerState`, and\n   `voteOutbox` only after authenticated handoff plus the existing rollback,\n   availability, and outbox gates. Those four stores enter the D.110c-c/d\n   bounded durable census. This is a high-risk security-authority slice and\n   closes before D.110c-a RED.\n2. **D.110c-0b0 — authenticated freshness-floor authority prerequisite.**\n   Owner: protocol-v3 trust opening, the creator signing/seal custody boundary,\n   Node cold reopen and product-room ownership, with storage, keychain,\n   availability, and Phase-7 bootstrap as candidate boundaries rather than\n   assumed authorities. The completed source audit proves there is no current\n   production-owned `(objectId, epoch, currentAnchorDigest)` floor that survives\n   restart independently of the untrusted generation being opened. A\n   creator-signed checkpoint authenticates content but cannot prove latestness\n   after complete rollback. The design checkpoint must compare at least a\n   monotonic creator-signing-custody floor, an application/account-held room\n   pin, and an external checkpoint/availability authority, and must state the\n   exact trust root, durable/atomic update law, crash behavior, rollback and\n   recovery law, migration, browser feasibility, public/API/schema impact, and\n   brand-new-client boundary, per-room O(1) growth law, and authenticated\n   rollback-safe retirement law. The selected floor owner must be named in the\n   D.110c-c/d durable censuses. No candidate is silently selected by this audit.\n   RED uses one genuine 0→1 transition, then presents a complete valid older\n   room generation from hostile storage and proves current production has no\n   authenticated input with which to reject it. GREEN must supply one reviewed\n   production owner and require exact equality between its authenticated room\n   floor and only the proposed current room head\n   `(objectId, epoch, currentAnchorDigest)`, rather than maximum-epoch\n   selection. Every genuine authenticated room-head installation must advance\n   that floor atomically with the durable head; the floor may advance only with\n   that head. A floor behind the authenticated durable room head, valid stale\n   or forked checkpoint, or floor rollback fails closed before activation or\n   deletion and may never authorize a generation merely because that\n   generation equals the lagging floor. The retained immediate predecessor is\n   lineage-authenticated under the separate bounded rule above and does not\n   match the latest floor. GREEN must recover safely across every crash edge. A\n   floor stored only beside the hostile checkpoint is invalid. Brand-new-client freshness remains a\n   separately reviewed Phase-7 authority. If the selected construction needs a\n   public API, new durable store/field, keychain authority, external service,\n   migration, dependency, or changed genesis assumption, 0b0 records that\n   exact high-risk implementation boundary and receives Grok/Kimi/Opus review\n   before production edits. Its design deadline is before 0a production edits;\n   its GREEN deadline is before 0b1 RED.\n3. **D.110c-0b — bounded authenticated trust checkpoint and control-proof\n   compaction decision.** Owner: protocol-v3/control-plane trust and closure\n   law, Node creator close, hot adoption verify/commit, cold reopen, and existing\n   durable owners as boundaries. The design\n   must permit age-independent cold reopen from the pinned genesis while\n   bounding the active control closure and without trusting storage bytes,\n   retaining an O(N) proof chain, weakening the genesis pin, or moving growth\n   into an uncounted store. Current trust records, live-journal anchor carriers,\n   and retained cut/QC evidence are inputs to the audit. The comparative audit\n   selects the existing-v1 dual-anchor creator checkpoint plus two rollback\n   generations and rejects WRAPS, an external pin, and a Merkle proof as the\n   authority solution, conditional on D.110c-0b0 supplying the missing floor.\n   D.110c-0b1 owns its reviewed opener/closure contract, exact\n   authenticated-head input, compatibility transition, RED/GREEN, and\n   stop boundary; it adds no new record/wire schema unless another high-risk\n   reslice is first accepted. Its executable RED waits for 0b0/0a/a/b GREEN and\n   consumes their real transition loop; it closes before D.110c-c RED and\n   D.110c-d freeze.\n4. **D.110c-0c — durable pending-adoption resume decision.** Owner: creator\n   close/adoption durable orchestration and existing AHE/snapshot/evidence\n   owners. RED must create a real closed durable head, terminate the process\n   before adoption, and prove no genuine reopen/resume entry exists. GREEN, if\n   separately approved, reconstructs only authenticated close/adoption facts\n   and resumes the existing verify/CAS/activate path; it never treats a pending\n   head as adopted. A new public entry or authority carrier is explicitly\n   reviewed, not smuggled into D.110c-c. It closes before the post-close and\n   pre-adoption restart gates and executes only after D.110c-0b1 GREEN.\n5. **D.110c-a — authenticated repeat-close state carrier.** Owner:\n   `packages/node/src/creator-close.ts` and the private installed-v3 close\n   registration in `v3-live.ts`, with compaction as unchanged verifier and 0a\n   as prerequisite. RED binds a real adopted epoch-1 plane and calls the genuine\n   close path; after isolating the 0a seal refusal, it fails because the exact\n   prior accumulator is unavailable/mismatched. GREEN creates one authenticated\n   epoch-continuity owner in prepared/live registration state, supplies it to\n   existing history derivation, and generalizes the exported close-result epoch\n   fields under a separately pinned public contract. Root/size mismatch, reset,\n   substitution, same-anchor double close, stale handle, and duplicate-close\n   mutants fail closed. This slice owns the epoch-N snapshot-substitution proof:\n   a snapshot or manifest from any earlier epoch/anchor must fail before state,\n   ACL, or recovery ownership changes. Every generalized genuine close that\n   durably installs a new authenticated room head must invoke the accepted 0b0\n   atomic floor-advance law; omitting or lagging that advance fails closed.\n   Deadline: before D.110c-b RED.\n6. **D.110c-b — general hot adoption, activation, and product custody.** Owner:\n   creator adoption/commit/activate modules, `creator-successor-live`,\n   installed-v3 handoff, and `examples/v3-room`. RED executes real 0→1 and\n   attempts 1→2; it pins first-transition projection constants, the topic-keyed\n   stale-wrapper success, missing successor close rebinding, and the room's\n   one-transition adoption latch. GREEN uses authenticated epochs/kinds,\n   replaces active ownership only for the exact next anchor, rebinds close after\n   every adoption, and permits the room to request the next genuine adoption.\n   Every generalized adoption/activation must observe the accepted 0b0 room\n   floor at the newly installed head; it cannot activate against a missing,\n   lagging, or independently advanced floor.\n   Existing first-transition APIs and epoch-1 governance controls remain valid;\n   new epoch-N controls are additive. Deadline: before D.110c-c RED.\n7. **D.110c-c — bounded cold reopen, repeated cleanup, restart, and custody.**\n   Owner: the existing D.109 cleanup/reclamation orchestrators and installed-v3\n   registration custody, consuming approved 0b/0c designs. RED/Green cover\n   fresh-process pre-close, post-close, pre-adoption, post-adoption, and\n   post-prune boundaries; age-independent trust reopen; offline/rebase outbox;\n   sequential prune plus one skipped-prune catch-up; two rollback generations;\n   availability refusal; receipt replay/staleness; active-owner uniqueness;\n   predecessor terminalization; bounded hot/control closure; and bounded live\n   registration/close ownership through at least three genuine transitions. Its\n   exact durable census includes browser `sealEvidence`, `voteSlots`,\n   `signerState`, and `voteOutbox` rows by epoch plus the selected 0b0 freshness\n   floor owner, and proves their authenticated, rollback-safe retirement rather\n   than merely counting AHE generations.\n   Physical store deletion authority remains unchanged unless a separate RED\n   demonstrates an owner defect. Deadline: before D.110c-d RED.\n8. **D.110c-d — retained long-horizon and golden-path prerequisite gate.**\n   Owner: tests and test/build infrastructure only after D.110c-a/b/c GREEN. One\n   room performs at least 100 genuine authenticated transitions. The exact bound\n   remains 100 unless this high-risk plan review approves another explicit value\n   before RED. It runs no adaptive retry and cannot replace real close/adopt\n   calls with synthetic maintenance. The retained durable census includes all\n   four creator-seal browser stores, the selected 0b0 freshness-floor owner,\n   and the Node control owners. Deadline:\n   before Phase-6 exit or Phase-7a execution.\n\n##### D.110c-0a bounded epoch-relative seal-custody plan\n\nThe completed source audit pins the exact defect and prevents a broader seal\nredesign. `packages/protocol-v3/src/seal.ts` holds `SealAuthorityState.epoch` as\nliteral `0`, rejects genuine creator trust when `material.currentEpoch !== 0`,\nand registers every authority identity at epoch 0. `packages/seal` repeats that\nliteral in voter enrollment, vote/round-change intent, pacemaker event, and\ncreator-close evidence types. The browser `voteSlots`, `signerState`,","numLines":470,"startLine":91568,"totalLines":93334}}}
