+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Nyej3MXZRib2nifuCz9iV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Exact-weight TSS\n2\t\n3\tA **threshold signature scheme (TSS)** lets a group of parties collectively sign a message by aggregating their\n4\tpartial signatures. The **access structure** of a TSS is the set of subsets of the parties whose partial signatures\n5\tcan be combined to form a valid signature. For an aBFT proof-of-stake blockchain, the ideal access structure at any\n6\tpoint in the protocol includes any set of nodes holding at least 1/3 of the total consensus weight. In a Hiero network,\n7\tconsensus weights are the whole number of HBARs staked to each node, and hence require 64-bit precision since each\n8\tnetwork has a fixed supply of 50B HBAR.\n9\t\n10\tIgnoring the amount of work and party interactions required of a verifier, it is relatively straightforward to design\n11\tsuch a TSS. It is much harder to design an **efficient** TSS with this property. Efficiency demands,\n12\t- A stable, succinct **ledger identity** that verifiers can trust during any period since adoption of the TSS in which\n13\tthe network's aBFT properties are not compromised; no matter how many nodes are added or removed, or change their\n14\tsigning keys.\n15\t- **Concise signatures** whose length is independent of the size of the network.\n16\t- **Fixed-cost, non-interactive verification** that is again independent of the size of the network.\n17\t\n18\tHiero uses an efficient exact-weight TSS where signing works via the **hinTS** scheme published in [1], and the\n19\tledger's identity is the hash of the ids, weights, and Schnorr public keys of the permissioned nodes in the network\n20\tat the time the TSS was adopted.\n21\t\n22\tThis document provides the high-level design of how Hiero TSS is implemented.\n23\t\n24\t### Relation to the `RosterService` lifecycle\n25\t\n26\tSince the very identity of the ledger in Hiero TSS depends on the ids, weights, and Schnorr keys of the nodes in\n27\tthe network roster at the time of adoption, the TSS implementation is naturally guided by the lifecycle of the\n28\t`RosterService`. This lifecycle has three phases:\n29\t1. **Bootstrap**: Only a genesis roster exists, and although the nodes have secure communication channels,\n30\tthere is no consensus state with the Schnorr or hinTS public keys of the nodes. In this phase, the TSS system must\n31\tderive this state from gossip; finish preprocessing for the hinTS scheme; and prove the initial hinTS verification\n32\tkey and weight threshold are embedded as metadata at the start of the chain of rosters blessed by Schnorr signatures\n33\tfrom nodes in the previous roster with at least 1/3 of the weight. (Since bootstrap begins the chain, verifiers must\n34\ttrust the genesis roster's members for prior reasons.)\n35\t2. **Transition**: The current roster is fully active; the TSS state includes a consensus proof that the roster,\n36\tits hinTS verification key, and minimum weight threshold were all blessed by a previous roster. There is also a\n37\tcandidate roster that reflects the latest dynamic address book changes and HBAR stake adjustments. In this phase, the\n38\tTSS system must repeat hinTS preprocessing for the candidate roster; and collect enough Schnorr signatures from the\n39\tcurrent roster to (recursively) prove _that_ roster's hinTS construction and minimum weight threshold.\n40\t3. **Handoff**: The current roster is fully active, but there is no candidate roster because the network has just\n41\treplaced its current roster with the candidate roster. In this phase, the TSS system may, for example, purge any\n42\tobsolete state accumulated during its previous transition phases; but it has no urgent responsibilities besides, of\n43\tcourse, providing ledger signatures.\n44\t\n45\t### The `RosterCompanionService` abstraction\n46\t\n47\tWe structure the TSS system as two services with distinct responsibilities:\n48\t1. The `HintsService`, which implements the six main hinTS algorithms that function given the **common reference\n49\tstring (CRS)** grounding the protocol; that is, preprocessing, key generation, hint generation, partial signing,\n50\tpartial verification, and signature aggregation.\n51\t2. The `HistoryService`, which is unaware of its role in specifically TSS, and implements a more general function of\n52\tproving via recursive SNARK that a set of node ids, weights, Schnorr keys; and some arbitrary metadata, were derived\n53\tfrom the ledger id with each transition in the derivation having valid Schnorr signatures from nodes with at least 1/3\n54\tof the weight in the source roster.\n55\t\n56\tThe node software combines these services to achieve TSS by setting the metadata in the `HistoryService` proof\n57\tfor each roster to the concatenation of two items:\n58\t1. The verification key computed by the `HintsService` for that roster.\n59\t2. The minimum threshold weight that a verifier should accept for a signature by this\n60\tverification key; in particular, 1/3 of the total weight in the roster, which is _not_ necessarily 1/3 of\n61\t50B HBAR---unless all HBAR were staked to nodes in the roster.\n62\t\n63\t(Note the full message that nodes sign with their Schnorr keys in each `HistoryService` proof is the concatenation of\n64\tthe above metadata with the hash of a canonical serialization of the list of `(node id, weight, Schnorr key)` triples\n65\tdefining the next roster.)\n66\t\n67\tDespite their separate responsibilities, both the `HintsService` and `HistoryService` share a high-level design that\n68\twe call the `RosterCompanionService`. This abstraction is a service whose goal is to derive some **primary state** for\n69\teach roster used by the `RosterService`, and that progresses, round by round, toward this goal by a **reconciliation\n70\tloop** driven by the `HandleWorkflow`. It uses whatever **secondary state** it needs and gossips any\n71\t**node transactions** required to implement this reconciliation loop.\n72\t\n73\tA `RosterCompanionService` is **ready** when it has derived its primary state for the current roster. Becoming ready is\n74\tthe prime objective of any `RosterCompanionService` during the bootstrap phase.\n75\t\n76\t![RCS bootstrap schematic](assets/rcs-bootstrap-schematic.png)\n77\t\n78\tCompleting bootstrap is equivalent to starting in a handoff phase, where a `RosterCompanionService` (`RCS`) has no\n79\tprimary state to derive. Its only required function is to do **roster-scoped work** for the current roster on behalf of\n80\tother protocol infrastructure components; though it may also use the reconciliation loop to purge obsolete state.\n81\t\n82\t![RCS handoff schematic](assets/rcs-handoff-schematic.png)\n83\t\n84\tAt distinguished points (e.g., at a stake period boundary), the Hiero protocol creates a candidate roster that reflects\n85\tdynamic address book changes and HBAR stake adjustments. This initiates a transition phase, where the `RCS` must use the\n86\treconciliation loop to derive its primary state for the candidate roster, at the same time it is doing tasks scoped to\n87\tthe current roster.\n88\t\n89\t![RCS transition schematic](assets/rcs-transition-schematic.png)\n90\t\n91\t#### A note on performance\n92\t\n93\tIn the first release of Hiero TSS, revisions to the candidate roster only happened at consensus midnight; and roster\n94\thandoffs only happened at upgrade boundaries scheduled 12 or more hours after UTC midnight. So new nodes after a handoff\n95\thad an entire release to successfully gossip their hinTS and Schnorr keys; and there were many hours between the latest\n96\trevision to the candidate roster and the next handoff to complete the work for each transition.\n97\t\n98\tIf Hiero networks move to a much more compressed schedule for roster revisions and transitions, `RCS` implementations\n99\tmay want to do some transition phase work preemptively, even before the next revision to the candidate roster.For\n100\texample,\n101\t- A node new to the network could estimate the size of the next candidate roster and preemptively publish its hinTS\n102\tkey for that size.\n103\t- A node new to the network could immediately publish its Schnorr key when going `ACTIVE`.\n104\t\n105\tBut these optimizations were not necessary in the first release of Hiero TSS.\n106\t\n107\t### The `HintsService` as a `RosterCompanionService`\n108\t\n109\tWe first map the `HintsService` to the `RCS` abstraction. Its **primary state** for each roster is a\n110\t**hinTS construction** with everything needed for deterministic progress toward the hinTS scheme for that roster.\n111\tThe main features of a hinTS construction are items such as,\n112\t1. A unique numeric id $c$ for the construction to help connect related hinTS state like votes to the construction.\n113\t2. The source roster hash.\n114\t3. The target roster hash (needed by a reconnecting node to recover weight information for nodes, and hence parties,\n115\tin the ongoing construction).\n116\t4. If the construction is complete, the hinTS aggregation and verification keys for the scheme.\n117\t5. If the construction is complete, the final mapping of node ids to hinTS party ids in the target roster.\n118\t6. If the construction is still collecting hinTS keys, the consensus time for the next aggregation attempt (an\n119\t**attempt** is a deterministic point in consensus time at which honest nodes pause and evaluate if they have enough\n120\thinTS keys to satisfy the weight inequalities discussed in the [section](#implied-limitations-on-the-transition-phase)\n121\tbelow and begin the hinTS preprocessing algorithm).\n122\t7. If the construction has stopped accepting hinTS keys, the adoption time of the last accepted hinTS key.\n123\t\n124\tNote that despite the strong functional analogy between the constructions that make up the roster-scoped primary state\n125\tfor the `HintsService` and `HistoryService`, they have quite distinct goal states and need not have a one-to-one\n126\tcorrespondence. In particular, even if the Hiero signing mechanism were to change from hinTS to something else, this\n127\twould not require any change to the structure of a `HistoryService` proof construction.\n128\t\n129\tThe `HintsService` **secondary state** is everything else needed to facilitate deterministic progress on a\n130\tconstruction; in particular, for nodes that reconnect during the construction. Main types of secondary state are\n131\titems such as,\n132\t1. _Per construction size `M = 2^k`_ : For as many parties as possible, for a party with id $i \\in [0, M)$, the\n133\tparty's hinTS key; the node id that submitted that hinTS key; the consensus time the hinTS key was adopted in the\n134\tongoing construction; and, if applicable, a revised hinTS key the same node wishes to use in subsequent constructions\n135\tof size $M$. (A node operator might wish to rotate their key for security reasons and trigger such a revision.)\n136\tNote that a node's assigned party id for a particular construction size never changes; so this implies such secondary\n137\tstate is fully purged before reusing a party id for a new node id.\n138\t2. _Per construction id `c`_ : For a subset of node ids $\\{ i_1, \\ldots, i_n \\}$ in the source roster of\n139\tconstruction $c$ accounting for at least 1/3 of its weight, their consensus vote for a particular preprocessing\n140\toutput with aggregation and verification keys for construction $c$.\n141\t\n142\tThe **reconciliation loop** of the `HintsService` evolves this secondary state by a combination of scheduling\n143\texpensive cryptographic operations to run off the `handleTransaction` thread, and gossiping the results of these\n144\toperations (or votes on those results) to other nodes. These node operations likely include,\n145\t1. `HintsKeyPublication` - a transaction publishing the node's hinTS key for a particular construction size for\n146\tuse in the next construction of that size (or an ongoing construction of that size, if this is the first such\n147\tpublication for the node). In general, storing node's hinTS keys for multiple party sizes may lead to some mild\n148\tduplication of information about the node's public BLS key; but this will be rounding error compared to the size of\n149\tthe hints, which are proportional to the number of parties.\n150\t2. `HintsPreprocessingVote` - a transaction publishing the node's vote for a particular preprocessing output for\n151\ta certain construction id; that is, the `(aggregation key, verification key)` output of the hinTS preprocessing\n152\talgorithm.\n153\t\n154\tThe `HintsService` is **ready** when it has completed a hinTS construction for the current roster.\n155\t\n156\tThe **roster-scoped work** of the `HintsService` is to accept a message (generally a block hash) and return a\n157\tfuture that resolves to the hinTS signature on the message. This will require gossiping partial signatures via\n158\ta `HintsPartialSignature` node transaction so the node can run the hinTS aggregation algorithm to produce a succinct\n159\tsignature for a set of partial signatures whose parties are nodes with at least 1/3 weight in the current roster.\n160\t\n161\t### The `HistoryService` as a `RosterCompanionService`\n162\t\n163\tNext we map the `HistoryService` to the `RosterCompanionService` abstraction. Its **primary state** has one global\n164\tentry, the ledger id. Its roster-scoped primary state is a **proof construction** with everything needed for\n165\tdeterministic progress toward the proof that this roster extends the chain of trust, incorporating any requested\n166\tmetadata for the roster. The main features of a proof construction are items such as,\n167\t1. An id $c$ for the construction to help connect related proof state like votes to the construction.\n168\t2. The source roster hash; and if this is not the ledger id, the proof from the ledger id to the source roster.\n169\t3. The target roster hash.\n170\t4. If the construction is complete, the proof that the target roster and metadata belong to the chain of trust.\n171\t5. If the construction is waiting on Schnorr keys, the consensus time for the next attempt to assemble the next\n172\t`(roster, metadata)` pair in the chain of trust.\n173\t6. If the construction is no longer accepting Schnorr keys, the consensus final assembly time for the next\n174\t`(roster, metadata)` pair in the chain of trust.\n175\t7. If the construction is complete, the proof extending the chain of trust to the target roster and metadata.\n176\t\n177\tThe **secondary state** of the `HistoryService` is everything needed to facilitate deterministic progress on a\n178\tconstruction; in particular, for nodes that reconnect during the construction. This includes items such as,\n179\t1. _Per node id `i`_ : The node's Schnorr key; the node's consensus time for the Schnorr key; and, if applicable, a\n180\trevised Schnorr key the same node wishes to use in subsequent constructions.\n181\t2. _Per construction id `c`_ : For a subset of node ids $\\{ i_1, \\ldots, i_n \\}$ in the source roster of construction\n182\t$c$ accounting for at least 1/3 of its weight, their signatures on a particular metadata and roster derivation for\n183\tconstruction $c$.\n184\t3. _Per construction id `c`_ : For a subset of node ids $\\{ i_1, \\ldots, i_n \\}$ in the source roster of construction\n185\t$c$ accounting for at least 1/3 of its weight, their consensus vote for a particular proof output for construction $c$.\n186\t\n187\tThe **reconciliation loop** of the `HistoryService` evolves this secondary state by a combination of scheduling\n188\texpensive cryptographic operations to run off the `handleTransaction` thread, and gossiping the results of these\n189\toperations (or votes on those results) to other nodes. These node operations likely include,\n190\t1. `HistoryProofKeyPublication` - a transaction publishing the node's Schnorr key for use in the next construction.\n191\t2. `HistoryProofSignature` - a transaction publishing the node's signature for the proof of a particular history (that\n192\tis, address book and associated metadata).\n193\tmetadata and roster concatenation for a certain construction id.\n194\t3. `HistoryProofVote` - a transaction publishing the node's vote for a particular proof output for a certain\n195\tconstruction id.\n196\t\n197\tThe `HistoryService` is **ready** when it has completed a proof that the current roster and metadata were derived from\n198\tthe ledger id.\n199\t\n200\tThe **roster-scoped work** of the `HistoryService` is to accept a byte string which must match the metadata of the\n201\tcurrent roster, and return the proof that this `(roster, metadata)` pair belongs to the chain of trust starting with\n202\tthe ledger id.\n203\t\n204\t### Integration with protocol components\n205\t\n206\tThe TSS system is then just the combination of the `HintsService` and `HistoryService` with the `RosterService`; with\n207\tthe `HistoryService` metadata always set to the verification key of the `HintsService` for the current roster,\n208\tconcatenated to the weight threshold $T$ required for signatures with that key. The complete ledger signature\n209\t$S_L$ on a message $\\textrm{msg}$ thus combines a hinTS signature $S_h$ with proven threshold $\\mathcal{T_S}$ at\n210\tleast a claimed minimum threshold $\\mathcal{T}$ on $\\textrm{msg}$ with a proof $P_{\\textrm{vk},\\mathcal{T}}$ that\n211\tthe pair $(\\textrm{vk}, \\mathcal{T})$ is exactly the metadata in a chain of trusted `(roster, metadata)` pairs\n212\textending from a well-known ledger id `I`. That is,\n213\t\n214\t$$ S_L[\\textrm{msg}] = (S_h[\\textrm{msg}], P_{\\textrm{vk},\\mathcal{T}})$$\n215\t\n216\tAn verifier of the signatures of a Hiero network will then,\n217\t- Validate out-of-band through some means that `I` is, in fact, the ledger id of the Hiero network in question.\n218\t- Deploy a generic hinTS verifier, *extended with* the ability to verify proofs asserting a chain-of-trust from `I`.\n219\t- Given a signature $S_L$ on $\\textrm{msg}$ message $\\textrm{msg}$, first check that $P_{\\textrm{vk},\\mathcal{T}}$ is\n220\tvalid, and hence the hinTS scheme producing $S_h$ was in fact produced by nodes inheriting a chain of trust starting\n221\tfrom the nodes that established the trusted ledger id. Second, verify that $S_h$ is a valid hinTS signature on\n222\t$\\textrm{msg}$.\n223\t\n224\tThere are only a few other details; namely,\n225\t- The `HandleWorkflow` is responsible for driving the reconciliation loops of both companion services.\n226\t- The `IngestWorkflow` must reject user transactions during the bootstrap phase.\n227\t- The `BlockStreamManager` must include every round in the genesis block until the TSS system is ready. (And thus it\n228\tmust be possible to tell the platform not to use any of these rounds for state saving or reconnect.)\n229\t\n230\t#### Implied limitations on the transition phase\n231\t\n232\tConsider a transition from roster $A$ with nodes $\\{A_1, \\ldots, A_n\\}$ and hinTS aggregation key $\\textrm{ak}_A$ to\n233\ta candidate roster $B$ with nodes $\\{B_1, \\ldots, B_m\\}$ and hinTS aggregation key $\\textrm{ak}_B$.\n234\t\n235\tFurthermore, let\n236\t$$w_A = \\sum_{i=1}^n w(A_i)$$\n237\tbe the total weight of roster $A$, and similarly for $B$. Also,\n238\t- Let $SA \\subset A$ be the set of nodes in $A$ that have a Schnorr signature on the\n239\tpair $(B, \\textrm{ak}_B)$.\n240\t- Let $SB \\subset B$ be the set of nodes in $B$ that have a Schnorr key in $B$.\n241\t- Let $HB \\subset B$ be the set of nodes in $B$ that have a hinTS key in $\\textrm{ak}_B$.\n242\t\n243\tNow, to preserve aBFT security, we must have Schnorr signatures on the pair $(B, \\textrm{ak}\\_B)$ from at least 1/3 of\n244\tthe weight in $A$. That is,\n245\t$$w\\_{SA} = \\sum\\_{A\\_i \\in {SA}} w(A\\_i) \\geq \\frac{1}{3} w\\_A$$\n246\t\n247\tThis implies that at least one honest node with non-zero weight signed off on the transition.\n248\t\n249\tHowever, there are two further conditions that `(B, m)` must satisfy for the network to remain operational after\n250\tadopting it.\n251\t\n252\tFirst, $HB$ must contain strictly more than 2/3 of the weight in $B$, so that even if just less\n253\tthan 1/3 of the weight in $B$ is malicious, there will remain 1/3 honest weight that can construct hinTS signatures\n254\tand keep signing blocks.\n255\t\n256\t$$w_{HB} = \\sum_{B_i \\in HB} w(B_i) > \\frac{2}{3} w_B$$\n257\t\n258\tSecond, we must again have\n259\t\n260\t$$w_{SB} = \\sum_{B_i \\in SB} w(B_i) > \\frac{2}{3} w_B$$\n261\t\n262\tso that again, even if just less than 1/3 of the weight in $B$ is malicious, there will remain at least 1/3 honest weight that\n263\tcan sign off on the next transition. (It is, of course, not necessary that $SB = HB$; only that these weight conditions\n264\tare satisfied.)\n265\t\n266\t**Important:** Given the current set of Hiero admin transactions, and the requirement that staking elections be\n267\tdone to only active nodes, it is not possible to satisfy the above inequalities on $SB$ and $\\mathbb{B}$\n268\twhile changing more than 1/3 of the weight in the network in a single transition.\n269\t\n270\t## References\n271\t\n272\t1. Garg, S., Jain, A., Mukherjee, P., Sinha, R., Wang, M., & Zhang, Y. (2023). *hinTS: Threshold Signatures with Silent\n273\t   Setup*. Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2023/567. Retrieved from [https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567](https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567)\n274\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b68b30f-133f-4078-a9a7-ee7aeeec8953","uuid":"690a3a69-33f7-461d-a2cd-f9af2ceb6c35","timestamp":"2026-09-02T08:25:49.596Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/exact-weight-tss.md","content":"## Exact-weight TSS\n\nA **threshold signature scheme (TSS)** lets a group of parties collectively sign a message by aggregating their\npartial signatures. The **access structure** of a TSS is the set of subsets of the parties whose partial signatures\ncan be combined to form a valid signature. For an aBFT proof-of-stake blockchain, the ideal access structure at any\npoint in the protocol includes any set of nodes holding at least 1/3 of the total consensus weight. In a Hiero network,\nconsensus weights are the whole number of HBARs staked to each node, and hence require 64-bit precision since each\nnetwork has a fixed supply of 50B HBAR.\n\nIgnoring the amount of work and party interactions required of a verifier, it is relatively straightforward to design\nsuch a TSS. It is much harder to design an **efficient** TSS with this property. Efficiency demands,\n- A stable, succinct **ledger identity** that verifiers can trust during any period since adoption of the TSS in which\nthe network's aBFT properties are not compromised; no matter how many nodes are added or removed, or change their\nsigning keys.\n- **Concise signatures** whose length is independent of the size of the network.\n- **Fixed-cost, non-interactive verification** that is again independent of the size of the network.\n\nHiero uses an efficient exact-weight TSS where signing works via the **hinTS** scheme published in [1], and the\nledger's identity is the hash of the ids, weights, and Schnorr public keys of the permissioned nodes in the network\nat the time the TSS was adopted.\n\nThis document provides the high-level design of how Hiero TSS is implemented.\n\n### Relation to the `RosterService` lifecycle\n\nSince the very identity of the ledger in Hiero TSS depends on the ids, weights, and Schnorr keys of the nodes in\nthe network roster at the time of adoption, the TSS implementation is naturally guided by the lifecycle of the\n`RosterService`. This lifecycle has three phases:\n1. **Bootstrap**: Only a genesis roster exists, and although the nodes have secure communication channels,\nthere is no consensus state with the Schnorr or hinTS public keys of the nodes. In this phase, the TSS system must\nderive this state from gossip; finish preprocessing for the hinTS scheme; and prove the initial hinTS verification\nkey and weight threshold are embedded as metadata at the start of the chain of rosters blessed by Schnorr signatures\nfrom nodes in the previous roster with at least 1/3 of the weight. (Since bootstrap begins the chain, verifiers must\ntrust the genesis roster's members for prior reasons.)\n2. **Transition**: The current roster is fully active; the TSS state includes a consensus proof that the roster,\nits hinTS verification key, and minimum weight threshold were all blessed by a previous roster. There is also a\ncandidate roster that reflects the latest dynamic address book changes and HBAR stake adjustments. In this phase, the\nTSS system must repeat hinTS preprocessing for the candidate roster; and collect enough Schnorr signatures from the\ncurrent roster to (recursively) prove _that_ roster's hinTS construction and minimum weight threshold.\n3. **Handoff**: The current roster is fully active, but there is no candidate roster because the network has just\nreplaced its current roster with the candidate roster. In this phase, the TSS system may, for example, purge any\nobsolete state accumulated during its previous transition phases; but it has no urgent responsibilities besides, of\ncourse, providing ledger signatures.\n\n### The `RosterCompanionService` abstraction\n\nWe structure the TSS system as two services with distinct responsibilities:\n1. The `HintsService`, which implements the six main hinTS algorithms that function given the **common reference\nstring (CRS)** grounding the protocol; that is, preprocessing, key generation, hint generation, partial signing,\npartial verification, and signature aggregation.\n2. The `HistoryService`, which is unaware of its role in specifically TSS, and implements a more general function of\nproving via recursive SNARK that a set of node ids, weights, Schnorr keys; and some arbitrary metadata, were derived\nfrom the ledger id with each transition in the derivation having valid Schnorr signatures from nodes with at least 1/3\nof the weight in the source roster.\n\nThe node software combines these services to achieve TSS by setting the metadata in the `HistoryService` proof\nfor each roster to the concatenation of two items:\n1. The verification key computed by the `HintsService` for that roster.\n2. The minimum threshold weight that a verifier should accept for a signature by this\nverification key; in particular, 1/3 of the total weight in the roster, which is _not_ necessarily 1/3 of\n50B HBAR---unless all HBAR were staked to nodes in the roster.\n\n(Note the full message that nodes sign with their Schnorr keys in each `HistoryService` proof is the concatenation of\nthe above metadata with the hash of a canonical serialization of the list of `(node id, weight, Schnorr key)` triples\ndefining the next roster.)\n\nDespite their separate responsibilities, both the `HintsService` and `HistoryService` share a high-level design that\nwe call the `RosterCompanionService`. This abstraction is a service whose goal is to derive some **primary state** for\neach roster used by the `RosterService`, and that progresses, round by round, toward this goal by a **reconciliation\nloop** driven by the `HandleWorkflow`. It uses whatever **secondary state** it needs and gossips any\n**node transactions** required to implement this reconciliation loop.\n\nA `RosterCompanionService` is **ready** when it has derived its primary state for the current roster. Becoming ready is\nthe prime objective of any `RosterCompanionService` during the bootstrap phase.\n\n![RCS bootstrap schematic](assets/rcs-bootstrap-schematic.png)\n\nCompleting bootstrap is equivalent to starting in a handoff phase, where a `RosterCompanionService` (`RCS`) has no\nprimary state to derive. Its only required function is to do **roster-scoped work** for the current roster on behalf of\nother protocol infrastructure components; though it may also use the reconciliation loop to purge obsolete state.\n\n![RCS handoff schematic](assets/rcs-handoff-schematic.png)\n\nAt distinguished points (e.g., at a stake period boundary), the Hiero protocol creates a candidate roster that reflects\ndynamic address book changes and HBAR stake adjustments. This initiates a transition phase, where the `RCS` must use the\nreconciliation loop to derive its primary state for the candidate roster, at the same time it is doing tasks scoped to\nthe current roster.\n\n![RCS transition schematic](assets/rcs-transition-schematic.png)\n\n#### A note on performance\n\nIn the first release of Hiero TSS, revisions to the candidate roster only happened at consensus midnight; and roster\nhandoffs only happened at upgrade boundaries scheduled 12 or more hours after UTC midnight. So new nodes after a handoff\nhad an entire release to successfully gossip their hinTS and Schnorr keys; and there were many hours between the latest\nrevision to the candidate roster and the next handoff to complete the work for each transition.\n\nIf Hiero networks move to a much more compressed schedule for roster revisions and transitions, `RCS` implementations\nmay want to do some transition phase work preemptively, even before the next revision to the candidate roster.For\nexample,\n- A node new to the network could estimate the size of the next candidate roster and preemptively publish its hinTS\nkey for that size.\n- A node new to the network could immediately publish its Schnorr key when going `ACTIVE`.\n\nBut these optimizations were not necessary in the first release of Hiero TSS.\n\n### The `HintsService` as a `RosterCompanionService`\n\nWe first map the `HintsService` to the `RCS` abstraction. Its **primary state** for each roster is a\n**hinTS construction** with everything needed for deterministic progress toward the hinTS scheme for that roster.\nThe main features of a hinTS construction are items such as,\n1. A unique numeric id $c$ for the construction to help connect related hinTS state like votes to the construction.\n2. The source roster hash.\n3. The target roster hash (needed by a reconnecting node to recover weight information for nodes, and hence parties,\nin the ongoing construction).\n4. If the construction is complete, the hinTS aggregation and verification keys for the scheme.\n5. If the construction is complete, the final mapping of node ids to hinTS party ids in the target roster.\n6. If the construction is still collecting hinTS keys, the consensus time for the next aggregation attempt (an\n**attempt** is a deterministic point in consensus time at which honest nodes pause and evaluate if they have enough\nhinTS keys to satisfy the weight inequalities discussed in the [section](#implied-limitations-on-the-transition-phase)\nbelow and begin the hinTS preprocessing algorithm).\n7. If the construction has stopped accepting hinTS keys, the adoption time of the last accepted hinTS key.\n\nNote that despite the strong functional analogy between the constructions that make up the roster-scoped primary state\nfor the `HintsService` and `HistoryService`, they have quite distinct goal states and need not have a one-to-one\ncorrespondence. In particular, even if the Hiero signing mechanism were to change from hinTS to something else, this\nwould not require any change to the structure of a `HistoryService` proof construction.\n\nThe `HintsService` **secondary state** is everything else needed to facilitate deterministic progress on a\nconstruction; in particular, for nodes that reconnect during the construction. Main types of secondary state are\nitems such as,\n1. _Per construction size `M = 2^k`_ : For as many parties as possible, for a party with id $i \\in [0, M)$, the\nparty's hinTS key; the node id that submitted that hinTS key; the consensus time the hinTS key was adopted in the\nongoing construction; and, if applicable, a revised hinTS key the same node wishes to use in subsequent constructions\nof size $M$. (A node operator might wish to rotate their key for security reasons and trigger such a revision.)\nNote that a node's assigned party id for a particular construction size never changes; so this implies such secondary\nstate is fully purged before reusing a party id for a new node id.\n2. _Per construction id `c`_ : For a subset of node ids $\\{ i_1, \\ldots, i_n \\}$ in the source roster of\nconstruction $c$ accounting for at least 1/3 of its weight, their consensus vote for a particular preprocessing\noutput with aggregation and verification keys for construction $c$.\n\nThe **reconciliation loop** of the `HintsService` evolves this secondary state by a combination of scheduling\nexpensive cryptographic operations to run off the `handleTransaction` thread, and gossiping the results of these\noperations (or votes on those results) to other nodes. These node operations likely include,\n1. `HintsKeyPublication` - a transaction publishing the node's hinTS key for a particular construction size for\nuse in the next construction of that size (or an ongoing construction of that size, if this is the first such\npublication for the node). In general, storing node's hinTS keys for multiple party sizes may lead to some mild\nduplication of information about the node's public BLS key; but this will be rounding error compared to the size of\nthe hints, which are proportional to the number of parties.\n2. `HintsPreprocessingVote` - a transaction publishing the node's vote for a particular preprocessing output for\na certain construction id; that is, the `(aggregation key, verification key)` output of the hinTS preprocessing\nalgorithm.\n\nThe `HintsService` is **ready** when it has completed a hinTS construction for the current roster.\n\nThe **roster-scoped work** of the `HintsService` is to accept a message (generally a block hash) and return a\nfuture that resolves to the hinTS signature on the message. This will require gossiping partial signatures via\na `HintsPartialSignature` node transaction so the node can run the hinTS aggregation algorithm to produce a succinct\nsignature for a set of partial signatures whose parties are nodes with at least 1/3 weight in the current roster.\n\n### The `HistoryService` as a `RosterCompanionService`\n\nNext we map the `HistoryService` to the `RosterCompanionService` abstraction. Its **primary state** has one global\nentry, the ledger id. Its roster-scoped primary state is a **proof construction** with everything needed for\ndeterministic progress toward the proof that this roster extends the chain of trust, incorporating any requested\nmetadata for the roster. The main features of a proof construction are items such as,\n1. An id $c$ for the construction to help connect related proof state like votes to the construction.\n2. The source roster hash; and if this is not the ledger id, the proof from the ledger id to the source roster.\n3. The target roster hash.\n4. If the construction is complete, the proof that the target roster and metadata belong to the chain of trust.\n5. If the construction is waiting on Schnorr keys, the consensus time for the next attempt to assemble the next\n`(roster, metadata)` pair in the chain of trust.\n6. If the construction is no longer accepting Schnorr keys, the consensus final assembly time for the next\n`(roster, metadata)` pair in the chain of trust.\n7. If the construction is complete, the proof extending the chain of trust to the target roster and metadata.\n\nThe **secondary state** of the `HistoryService` is everything needed to facilitate deterministic progress on a\nconstruction; in particular, for nodes that reconnect during the construction. This includes items such as,\n1. _Per node id `i`_ : The node's Schnorr key; the node's consensus time for the Schnorr key; and, if applicable, a\nrevised Schnorr key the same node wishes to use in subsequent constructions.\n2. _Per construction id `c`_ : For a subset of node ids $\\{ i_1, \\ldots, i_n \\}$ in the source roster of construction\n$c$ accounting for at least 1/3 of its weight, their signatures on a particular metadata and roster derivation for\nconstruction $c$.\n3. _Per construction id `c`_ : For a subset of node ids $\\{ i_1, \\ldots, i_n \\}$ in the source roster of construction\n$c$ accounting for at least 1/3 of its weight, their consensus vote for a particular proof output for construction $c$.\n\nThe **reconciliation loop** of the `HistoryService` evolves this secondary state by a combination of scheduling\nexpensive cryptographic operations to run off the `handleTransaction` thread, and gossiping the results of these\noperations (or votes on those results) to other nodes. These node operations likely include,\n1. `HistoryProofKeyPublication` - a transaction publishing the node's Schnorr key for use in the next construction.\n2. `HistoryProofSignature` - a transaction publishing the node's signature for the proof of a particular history (that\nis, address book and associated metadata).\nmetadata and roster concatenation for a certain construction id.\n3. `HistoryProofVote` - a transaction publishing the node's vote for a particular proof output for a certain\nconstruction id.\n\nThe `HistoryService` is **ready** when it has completed a proof that the current roster and metadata were derived from\nthe ledger id.\n\nThe **roster-scoped work** of the `HistoryService` is to accept a byte string which must match the metadata of the\ncurrent roster, and return the proof that this `(roster, metadata)` pair belongs to the chain of trust starting with\nthe ledger id.\n\n### Integration with protocol components\n\nThe TSS system is then just the combination of the `HintsService` and `HistoryService` with the `RosterService`; with\nthe `HistoryService` metadata always set to the verification key of the `HintsService` for the current roster,\nconcatenated to the weight threshold $T$ required for signatures with that key. The complete ledger signature\n$S_L$ on a message $\\textrm{msg}$ thus combines a hinTS signature $S_h$ with proven threshold $\\mathcal{T_S}$ at\nleast a claimed minimum threshold $\\mathcal{T}$ on $\\textrm{msg}$ with a proof $P_{\\textrm{vk},\\mathcal{T}}$ that\nthe pair $(\\textrm{vk}, \\mathcal{T})$ is exactly the metadata in a chain of trusted `(roster, metadata)` pairs\nextending from a well-known ledger id `I`. That is,\n\n$$ S_L[\\textrm{msg}] = (S_h[\\textrm{msg}], P_{\\textrm{vk},\\mathcal{T}})$$\n\nAn verifier of the signatures of a Hiero network will then,\n- Validate out-of-band through some means that `I` is, in fact, the ledger id of the Hiero network in question.\n- Deploy a generic hinTS verifier, *extended with* the ability to verify proofs asserting a chain-of-trust from `I`.\n- Given a signature $S_L$ on $\\textrm{msg}$ message $\\textrm{msg}$, first check that $P_{\\textrm{vk},\\mathcal{T}}$ is\nvalid, and hence the hinTS scheme producing $S_h$ was in fact produced by nodes inheriting a chain of trust starting\nfrom the nodes that established the trusted ledger id. Second, verify that $S_h$ is a valid hinTS signature on\n$\\textrm{msg}$.\n\nThere are only a few other details; namely,\n- The `HandleWorkflow` is responsible for driving the reconciliation loops of both companion services.\n- The `IngestWorkflow` must reject user transactions during the bootstrap phase.\n- The `BlockStreamManager` must include every round in the genesis block until the TSS system is ready. (And thus it\nmust be possible to tell the platform not to use any of these rounds for state saving or reconnect.)\n\n#### Implied limitations on the transition phase\n\nConsider a transition from roster $A$ with nodes $\\{A_1, \\ldots, A_n\\}$ and hinTS aggregation key $\\textrm{ak}_A$ to\na candidate roster $B$ with nodes $\\{B_1, \\ldots, B_m\\}$ and hinTS aggregation key $\\textrm{ak}_B$.\n\nFurthermore, let\n$$w_A = \\sum_{i=1}^n w(A_i)$$\nbe the total weight of roster $A$, and similarly for $B$. Also,\n- Let $SA \\subset A$ be the set of nodes in $A$ that have a Schnorr signature on the\npair $(B, \\textrm{ak}_B)$.\n- Let $SB \\subset B$ be the set of nodes in $B$ that have a Schnorr key in $B$.\n- Let $HB \\subset B$ be the set of nodes in $B$ that have a hinTS key in $\\textrm{ak}_B$.\n\nNow, to preserve aBFT security, we must have Schnorr signatures on the pair $(B, \\textrm{ak}\\_B)$ from at least 1/3 of\nthe weight in $A$. That is,\n$$w\\_{SA} = \\sum\\_{A\\_i \\in {SA}} w(A\\_i) \\geq \\frac{1}{3} w\\_A$$\n\nThis implies that at least one honest node with non-zero weight signed off on the transition.\n\nHowever, there are two further conditions that `(B, m)` must satisfy for the network to remain operational after\nadopting it.\n\nFirst, $HB$ must contain strictly more than 2/3 of the weight in $B$, so that even if just less\nthan 1/3 of the weight in $B$ is malicious, there will remain 1/3 honest weight that can construct hinTS signatures\nand keep signing blocks.\n\n$$w_{HB} = \\sum_{B_i \\in HB} w(B_i) > \\frac{2}{3} w_B$$\n\nSecond, we must again have\n\n$$w_{SB} = \\sum_{B_i \\in SB} w(B_i) > \\frac{2}{3} w_B$$\n\nso that again, even if just less than 1/3 of the weight in $B$ is malicious, there will remain at least 1/3 honest weight that\ncan sign off on the next transition. (It is, of course, not necessary that $SB = HB$; only that these weight conditions\nare satisfied.)\n\n**Important:** Given the current set of Hiero admin transactions, and the requirement that staking elections be\ndone to only active nodes, it is not possible to satisfy the above inequalities on $SB$ and $\\mathbb{B}$\nwhile changing more than 1/3 of the weight in the network in a single transition.\n\n## References\n\n1. Garg, S., Jain, A., Mukherjee, P., Sinha, R., Wang, M., & Zhang, Y. (2023). *hinTS: Threshold Signatures with Silent\n   Setup*. Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2023/567. Retrieved from [https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567](https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567)\n","numLines":274,"startLine":1,"totalLines":274}}}
+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MhSdZ4VvZLkPX9tZP1Udnt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\thip: 1200\n3\ttitle: The hinTS threshold signature scheme \n4\tauthor: Michael Tinker <@tinker-michaelj>, Rohit Sinha <@rsinha> \n5\tworking-group: Edward Wertz <@edward-swirldslabs>, Neeha Sompalli <@Neeharika-Sompalli>\n6\trequested-by: Hashgraph\n7\ttype: Standards Track\n8\tcategory: Service\n9\tneeds-hiero-approval: Yes\n10\tneeds-hedera-review: Yes\n11\thedera-review-date: 2025-10-21\n12\thedera-acceptance-decision: Accepted \n13\tstatus: Approved\n14\tlast-call-date-time: 2025-10-01T07:00:00Z\n15\tcreated: 2025-04-15\n16\tdiscussions-to: https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/discussions/1181\n17\tupdated: 2025-10-15\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## Abstract\n21\tThis proposal introduces the hinTS threshold signature scheme (TSS), designed for Hiero networks to sign blocks within the block stream (HIP-1056). It replaces individual RSA signatures with constant-size, efficiently verifiable signatures that are seamlessly integrated into the block stream.\n22\t\n23\tCurrently Hiero networks use record signing in the V6 record stream to ensure integrity, as defined in HIP-415. Each node generates an individual RSA signature on the hash of every block it produces, verified independently by a majority of weighted nodes.\n24\t\n25\tThe current RSA-based signing is inefficient. Verifiers must verify RSA keys from multiple nodes  which is computationally costly and will grow as nodes are added to the network. With uniform stake distribution, signature count and verification work scale linearly with node count. Verifying numerous RSA signatures on EVM chains is impractical due to linear scaling and lack of native RSA precompile support.\n26\t\n27\tThis HIP proposes the adoption of TSS be introduced alongside HIP-1056’s block stream. TSS will produce a constant-size signature per block, simplifying verification for block nodes, mirror nodes and other services, including via EVM smart contracts with gas-efficient precompiles.\n28\t\n29\tTSS provides the following advantages:\n30\tConstant-Size Signatures: Signature size remains fixed, regardless of node count or weight distribution, reducing storage and transmission overhead.\n31\tConstant-Time Verification: Verification work is independent of network size, improving scalability and enabling efficient EVM integration.\n32\t\n33\t## Motivation\n34\tMost consumers of a Hiero network's block stream will need to verify the blocks they receive by checking\n35\tthe network's signature on the block Merkle tree. Many network clients will also want to verify state proofs\n36\tby checking the same signature (since the state Merkle tree is a subtree of the block Merkle tree). It follows\n37\tthat the cost of verifying block signatures has a large effect on a Hiero network's usability, _especially_\n38\tas the network size grows and its history includes many roster and weight changes. For example, suppose a\n39\tdecentralized finance (DeFi) application wants to respond as soon as possible to state changes that\n40\trepresent onchain market signals. It must be able to verify each block's signature quickly and cheaply.\n41\tAs another example, consider inter-ledger communication between Hiero networks and EVM chains. Any such\n42\tinter-ledger protocol will need to be based on an efficient threshold signature scheme to keep gas costs\n43\tlow.\n44\t\n45\t## Rationale\n46\tThe appeal of the hinTS scheme is described extensively in [1], so we will not repeat each\n47\treason to adopt it in this HIP. The main benefit we do want to emphasize, other than\n48\tefficiency, is that hinTS lets nodes be assigned **exact weights**, even at the resolution\n49\tof 64-bit stake weights. Moreover, it uses a silent setup, avoiding the communication and \n50\tcomputation heavy protocols often associated with DKG-based threshold signature schemes.\n51\t\n52\tThe support for exact weights is attractive because, for an aBFT proof-of-stake blockchain, the \n53\t**access structure** needed to form a network signature is the agreement of any set of \n54\tnodes holding greater than 1/2 of the total consensus weight. In a Hiero network, consensus \n55\tweights are the whole number of HBARs staked to each node, and the network's HBAR supply\n56\tcan be configured with 64-bit precision. It follows that the weights in a Hiero threshold\n57\taccess structure also need 64-bit precision.\n58\t\n59\tThus, adopting hinTS lets Hiero networks impose exactly the access structure that is\n60\tmost natural for the aggregate signatures of a proof-of-stake blockchain.\n61\t\n62\t## User stories\n63\t- As a Hiero network user, I want to be able to verify the signature of any block proof created \n64\tby the network _without_ keeping the full address book history and the signing (RSA) key of \n65\tevery node throughout that history.\n66\t- As a Hiero network user, I want to do constant-time work to verify each block proof.\n67\t- As a Hiero block node operator, I want concise network signatures to reduce the amount \n68\tof information I need to store and serve to block proof consumers.\n69\t\n70\t## Technical Overview\n71\tThe hinTS signature on any block `N` attests to the statement that the block's content has been signed (via the BLS \n72\talgorithm) by nodes constituting of greater than 1/2 of the network weight, according to the address book in use for block `N`. \n73\tHowever, since the network can adopt different address books over time, the statement above must also imply that \n74\tthe address book being used in block `N` is a descendent of the genesis address book, wherein each address \n75\tbook rotation is authorized via signatures of greater than 1/2 (in weight) of the members of the active address book.\n76\t\n77\tWe label each address book with a (public) verification key, which is used to verify that the aggregate BLS signature \n78\t(computed by aggregating the BLS partial signatures) is derived from a threshold fraction of the nodes by weight.\n79\tStarting with the genesis address book, each time the network adopts a new address book, it will publish a \n80\trecursive SNARK proof in the block stream that proves the new address book's verification key belongs to the \n81\tnetwork's **chain of trust** -- the witnesses to this recursive SNARK prover algorithm is the prior recursive \n82\tSNARK proof, and a set of Schnorr signatures from members of the prior address book, representing greater than 1/2 amount of weight.\n83\tTherefore, in hinTS, each block signature contains the following components: \n84\t  1. a BLS aggregate signature, resulting from combining the partial BLS signatures over that block;\n85\t  2. a lightweight SNARK proof proving that the signers in the above BLS aggregate signature comprised of greater than 1/2 of the active \n86\t     address book by weight -- this proof is verified w.r.t. the verification key of the active address book;\n87\t  3. a recursive SNARK proof proving that the active address book is a descendent of the genesis address book;\n88\t\n89\t## Specification\n90\tOur specification has four parts.\n91\t1. First, we define the TSS **address book** and how we can use **proof keys** to link one \n92\taddress book to the next in a cryptographic chain of trust that binds arbitrary **metadata** \n93\tto each address book in the chain. \n94\t2. Next, we outline the information flow in the construction of a hinTS scheme, emphasizing \n95\tthat the main external output of each hinTS scheme, its **verification key**, is exactly the \n96\tmetadata bound to the corresponding address book in the chain of trust.\n97\t3. Third, we discuss the special case of the genesis block that initializes the chain of trust.\n98\t4. Finally, we tie everything together by specifying the `BlockProof` protobuf message a\n99\tHiero network uses to externalize its block signatures.\n100\t\n101\t**Important:** This specification is for developers working on the core protocol, block nodes,\n102\tand mirror nodes. We confine ourselves to just what is needed to correctly produce and consume \n103\tthe TSS protocol outputs in the block stream, taking as given a cryptography library that \n104\tprovides the API of the `HintsLibrary` and `HistoryLibrary` interfaces in the reference \n105\timplementation in [4]. \n106\t\n107\tReaders interested in thoroughly understanding the reference implementation should refer to \n108\tits [design documentation](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hedera-node/docs/exact-weight-tss.md). \n109\tReaders interested in the deeper details of the cryptography should consult [1].\n110\t\n111\t### Schnorr proof keys and the address book chain of trust\n112\t\n113\tUsers of a Hiero network must apriori trust a large majority of the _originators_ of the network, \n114\twhich forms the root of trust rather than some cryptographic proof.\n115\tThis is obvious since the originators will, by definition, hold the entire stake weight; \n116\tand, even the gold standard of aBFT security is mathematically limited to a corruption threshold \n117\tof strictly less than a third of the weight.\n118\t\n119\tBut users should never have to extend their trust beyond the aforementioned root \n120\t(and the limits of aBFT fault tolerance and other cryptographic assumptions within hinTS). \n121\tEvery future change to the membership of the network must come with cryptographic proof that at least \n122\tone third of the weight (and hence at least one honest node) voted for that membership change.\n123\t\n124\tWe propose a recursive proof mechanism that uses a succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge\n125\t(SNARK) based on honest nodes contributing signatures to address book. These signatures are computed using \n126\tSchnorr private keys whose public keys are gossiped to the network and stored within the address book. \n127\tThe message used to gossip a Schnorr public key is,\n128\t```\n129\tmessage HistoryProofKeyPublicationTransactionBody {\n130\t  /**\n131\t   * The public key the submitting node intends to use when\n132\t   * contributing signatures for use in proving history\n133\t   * belongs to the chain of trust for the ledger id.\n134\t   */\n135\t  bytes history_public_key = 1;\n136\t} \n137\t```\n138\t\n139\tThe network membership is characterized by a **TSS address book** made up of `(nodeId, history_public_key, weight)` \n140\ttriples; where the `history_public_key`s are the Schnorr public keys gossiped as above; \n141\tand the `nodeId` and `weight` fields are identical to those in a current or candidate consensus roster.\n142\t\n143\tIt follows that every recursive proof construction derives from a **source roster** (and address book), \n144\tand aims to prove a trustworthy transition to a **target roster** (and address book). The first \n145\ttime that TSS is enabled on a Hiero network is a special case in which the same roster serves as \n146\tboth source _and_ target; the TSS address book for this special roster is called the **genesis \n147\taddress book**. From that point forward, the SHA-256 hash of the genesis address book is the\n148\t **ledger id**.\n149\t```\n150\tledgerId = <genesisAddressBookHash>\n151\t```\n152\t\n153\tIn the reference implementation, as soon as the ledger id is computed, it is set in a singleton\n154\twith the State API key `HistoryService.LEDGER_ID` with `StateIdentifier` ordinal\n155\t[`42`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L420).\n156\tThis singleton has the simple type,\n157\t```\n158\tmessage ProtoBytes {\n159\t    bytes value = 1;\n160\t}\n161\t```\n162\t\n163\tFor record stream legibility, when the ledger id is created the network also publishes a\n164\tnew synthetic transaction body,\n165\t```\n166\tmessage LedgerIdPublicationTransactionBody {\n167\t  /**\n168\t   * The new ledger id.\n169\t   */\n170\t  bytes ledger_id = 1;\n171\t\n172\t  /**\n173\t   * The key to use when verifying recursive chain-of-trust proofs that extend\n174\t   * from this ledger id.\n175\t   */\n176\t  bytes history_proof_verification_key = 2;\n177\t}\n178\t```\n179\twhere the `history_proof_verification_key` is the key used to verify the recursive proofs\n180\tthat extend the chain of trust from the ledger id through TSS address book changes.\n181\t\n182\tAny block stream consumer that wants to validate signatures should keep the ledger id\n183\treadily available, since it is an ingredient in every chain-of-trust proof.\n184\t\n185\tThe honest nodes in the network prove every subsequent address book change \n186\t--- with respect to the above ledger id --- by first using a deterministic \n187\tpolicy to determine the hash of the target address book, then gossiping their \n188\tSchnorr signatures on the hash of that address book after being\n189\t_concatenated with arbitrary metadata that should be trusted for that address book_. \n190\tThe message used to gossip these signatures is,\n191\t```\n192\tmessage HistoryProofSignatureTransactionBody {\n193\t  /**\n194\t   * The id of the proof construction this signature is contributing to.\n195\t   */\n196\t  uint64 construction_id = 1;\n197\t\n198\t  /**\n199\t   * This node's signature on its computed history.\n200\t   */\n201\t  com.hedera.hapi.node.state.history.HistorySignature signature = 2;\n202\t}\n203\t```\n204\t\n205\tWhere we have,\n206\t```\n207\t/**\n208\t * A piece of new history in the form of an address book hash and\n209\t * associated metadata.\n210\t */\n211\tmessage History {\n212\t  /**\n213\t   * The address book hash of the new history.\n214\t   */\n215\t  bytes address_book_hash = 1;\n216\t  /**\n217\t   * The metadata associated to the address book.\n218\t   */\n219\t  bytes metadata = 2;\n220\t}\n221\t\n222\t/**\n223\t * A node's signature blessing some new history.\n224\t */\n225\tmessage HistorySignature {\n226\t  /**\n227\t   * The new history the node is signing.\n228\t   */\n229\t  History history = 1;\n230\t  /**\n231\t   * The node's signature on the canonical serialization of\n232\t   * the new history.\n233\t   */\n234\t  bytes signature = 2;\n235\t}\n236\t```\n237\t\n238\tThe honest nodes then use another deterministic policy to take a sufficient set of the\n239\tvalid signatures as inputs to the recursive SNARK prover; compute the proof; and then \n240\tgossip their vote for that proof as the evidence the roster transition is trustworthy.\n241\t\n242\tThe arbitrary metadata concatenated with the address book is the bridge between the\n243\taddress book chain of trust and the hinTS signing scheme actually used to create TSS\n244\tsignatures, as we see next.\n245\t\n246\t### hinTS BLS keys and TSS signatures\n247\t\n248\tWhen faced with a new candidate address book, the honest nodes in the network must\n249\twork together to setup the hinTS scheme for that address book. The first step\n250\tis to gossip a special, cryptographically \"extended\" form of the public BLS key \n251\t-- called the hinTS key -- that they have generated for their individual use.\n252\t```\n253\tmessage HintsKeyPublicationTransactionBody {\n254\t  ...\n255\t  /**\n256\t   * The party's hinTS key.\n257\t   */\n258\t  bytes hints_key = 3;\n259\t}\n260\t```\n261\t\n262\tUnlike simple BLS keys, these extended hinTS keys have information that can be **preprocessed** \n263\tinto an aggregated **verification key**. Given a verification key `VK` that aggregates the \n264\thinTS keys `{ k_1, k_2, ..., k_n }`, the hinTS scheme lets us replace (1) a linear-size list \n265\tof partial signatures `{ s_1, s_2, ... s_n }` that verify under the respective keys; \n266\twith (2) a single aggregate signature `S` (with claimed aggregate weight) that verifies under `VK`.\n267\t\n268\tThe honest nodes use a deterministic policy to choose which of the published hinTS\n269\tkeys they will aggregate into the verification key for the target address book, then\n270\tgossip a vote on the resulting preprocessing output. The honest nodes adopt the `VK`\n271\tfrom the preprocessing output that receives votes from at least a third of the stake\n272\tweight in the source address book for the construction.\n273\t\n274\t### The genesis block\n275\t\n276\tWe discussed above how the `VK` for the hinTS scheme of an address book is included\n277\tin the chain-of-trust proof that links that address book to the previous one. However,\n278\tchain must have a beginning---that is, a genesis address book with an initial hinTS\n279\tscheme.\n280\t\n281\tFor the genesis hinTS `VK`, there is no recursive proof. The genesis block itself\n282\tmust be trusted. Specifically, the Hiero protocol guarantees the genesis block of\n283\ta network will include,\n284\t1. A `HistoryProofKeyPublicationTransactionBody` for strictly more than 2/3 of the\n285\tweight in the genesis roster.\n286\t2. The ledger id derived from the hash of the address book with these keys.\n287\t3. A `HistoryProofSignatureTransactionBody` on the `History` formed from this\n288\tledger id and the genesis hinTS `VK` for greater than 1/2 of the weight in the genesis\n289\troster.\n290\t\n291\tIf a stream consumer trusts it knows the ledger id for a network, it can then,\n292\tgiven the genesis block, verify the Schnorr keys and roster in that block hash\n293\tto the ledger id. Since this proves the Schnorr keys are trustworthy, their\n294\tsignatures in the genesis block then establish the genesis hinTS key.\n295\t\n296\t### Verifying `BlockProof`s as a stream consumer\n297\t\n298\tA Hiero network externalizes the proof of each block in the following message.\n299\t```\n300\tmessage BlockProof {\n301\t    /**\n302\t     * The block this proof secures.<br/>\n303\t     * We provide this because a proof for a future block can be used to prove\n304\t     * the state of the ledger at that block and the blocks before it.<br/>\n305\t     * <p>\n306\t     * This value SHOULD match the block number of the current block,\n307\t     * under normal operation.\n308\t     */\n309\t    uint64 block = 1;\n310\t\n311\t    /**\n312\t     * The hinTS key that this signature verifies under; a stream consumer should\n313\t     * only use this key after first checking the chain of trust proof.\n314\t     */\n315\t    bytes verification_key = 2;\n316\t\n317\t    /**\n318\t     * Proof the hinTS verification key is in the chain of trust extending\n319\t     * from the network's ledger id.\n320\t     */\n321\t    ChainOfTrustProof verification_key_proof = 3;\n322\t\n323\t    /**\n324\t     * The proof contents verifying the block's merkle root hash.<br/>\n325\t     * This is a `oneof` field that MAY contain one several types of\n326\t     * proofs.\n327\t     */\n328\t    oneof proof {\n329\t        /**\n330\t         * A TSS signature over the block's merkle root hash.<br/>\n331\t         * This signature SHALL be produced by a threshold signature scheme\n332\t         * that allows multiple nodes to contribute partial signatures that\n333\t         * can be aggregated into a single signature. This field MUST be\n334\t         * used when the current block is signed directly by the consensus\n335\t         * nodes with a TSS signature; otherwise it MUST be empty.\n336\t         */\n337\t        TssSignedBlockProof signed_block_proof = 4;\n338\t\n339\t        /**\n340\t         * A proof of the block merkle tree's contents. This proof SHALL\n341\t         * contain the information necessary to validate the previous block's\n342\t         * hash, along with any information necessary to validate the current\n343\t         * block's hash.\n344\t         * <p>\n345\t         * Of necessity, the state proof MUST contain a set of hash values,\n346\t         * along with ordering information, that allows for reconstruction of the\n347\t         * block's hash. This list of hash values form the set of sibling hash values\n348\t         * needed to correctly reconstruct the parent hash, and all hash values\n349\t         * \"above\" that hash in the merkle tree.\n350\t         * <p>\n351\t         * A Block proof can be constructed by combining the sibling hashes for\n352\t         * a previous block hash and sibling hashes for each entry \"above\" that\n353\t         * node in the merkle tree of a block proof that incorporates that previous\n354\t         * block hash. This form of block proof may be used to prove a chain of\n355\t         * blocks when one or more older blocks is missing the original block\n356\t         * proof that signed the block's merkle root directly.\n357\t         * <p>\n358\t         * Such a list MUST be ordered from the sibling of the node that contains\n359\t         * this block's root node hash, and continues up the merkle tree to the\n360\t         * root hash of the signed block proof.\n361\t         * <p>\n362\t         * If this block proof has a \"direct\" signature, then any associated list\n363\t         * of siblings MUST be empty.<br/>\n364\t         * If said list is not empty, then this block proof MUST be verified by\n365\t         * first constructing the \"block\" merkle tree and computing the root hash\n366\t         * of that tree, then combining that hash with the values in this list,\n367\t         * paying attention to the first/second sibling ordering, until the root\n368\t         * merkle hash is produced from the last pair of sibling hashes. That\n369\t         * \"secondary\" root hash MUST then be verified using the value of\n370\t         * `block_signature`.\n371\t         */\n372\t        StateProof block_state_proof = 5;\n373\t\n374\t        /**\n375\t         * A proof consisting of RSA signatures from consensus nodes.<br/>\n376\t         * This proof type exists for backward compatibility with blocks that\n377\t         * wrap historical record files.<br/>\n378\t         * This field MUST be set when the block wraps a record file signed by\n379\t         * individual RSA signatures from consensus nodes; otherwise it MUST be\n380\t         * empty.\n381\t         */\n382\t        SignedRecordFileProof signed_record_file_proof = 6;\n383\t    }\n384\t}\n385\t\n386\tmessage ChainOfTrustProof {\n387\t    oneof proof {\n388\t        /**\n389\t         * If there is not yet a SNARK proving the chain of trust from ledger id to\n390\t         * the hinTS verification key, an aggregation of Schnorr signatures on\n391\t         * the concatenation of the ledger id and genesis hinTS verification key\n392\t         * that serve as witnesses for the SNARK prover algorithm.\n393\t         */\n394\t        AggregatedNodeSignatures aggregated_node_signatures = 1;\n395\t        /**\n396\t         * If known, a ZK-compressed SNARK proof proving the chain of trust from\n397\t         * the ledger id to this hinTS verification key.\n398\t         */\n399\t        bytes wraps_proof = 2;\n400\t    }\n401\t}\n402\t\n403\t/**\n404\t * An aggregation of node signatures on some data.\n405\t * <p>\n406\t * Can be used to prove the genesis hinTS verification key in a block proof; but\n407\t * not succinct and not recursive; hence in normal operations with TSS, used only\n408\t * until the first recursive proof is available.\n409\t */\n410\tmessage AggregatedNodeSignatures {\n411\t  /**\n412\t   * The aggregated signature.\n413\t   */\n414\t  bytes aggregated_signature = 1;\n415\t  /**\n416\t   * In ascending order, the ids of the nodes that contributed signatures.\n417\t   */\n418\t  repeated uint64 signing_node_ids = 2;\n419\t}\n420\t\n421\t/**\n422\t * A proof containing a TSS signature for a single block.\n423\t */\n424\tmessage TssSignedBlockProof {\n425\t    /**\n426\t     * A TSS signature for one block.<br/>\n427\t     * This is a single signature representing the collection of partial\n428\t     * signatures from nodes holding strictly greater than 2/3 of the\n429\t     * current network \"weight\" in aggregate. The signature is produced by\n430\t     * cryptographic \"aggregation\" of the partial signatures to produce a\n431\t     * single signature that can be verified with the network public key,\n432\t     * but could not be produced by fewer nodes than required to meet the\n433\t     * threshold for network stake \"weight\".\n434\t     * <p>\n435\t     * This message MUST make use of a threshold signature scheme like `BLS`\n436\t     * which provides the necessary cryptographic guarantees.<br/>\n437\t     * This signature SHALL use a TSS signature to provide a single signature\n438\t     * that represents the consensus signature of consensus nodes.<br/>\n439\t     * The exact subset of nodes that signed SHALL neither be known nor\n440\t     * tracked, but it SHALL be cryptographically verifiable that the\n441\t     * threshold was met if the signature itself can be validated with\n442\t     * the network public key (a.k.a `LedgerID`).\n443\t     */\n444\t    bytes block_signature = 1;\n445\t}\n446\t\n447\tmessage StateProof {\n448\t    /**\n449\t     * The merkle paths that prove the nodes in the block merkle tree.\n450\t     * This field SHALL contain, in the following order:\n451\t     * - The merkle path from the previous block root (the \"left-most\"\n452\t     *   node on level 5) to it's (single) internal node parent (on level\n453\t     *   1, adjacent to the block's consensus timestamp).\n454\t     * - The merkle path from the consensus timestamp leaf to the root.\n455\t     * - The merkle path of the root of the entire block merkle tree.\n456\t     */\n457\t    repeated MerklePath paths = 1;\n458\t\n459\t    oneof proof {\n460\t        /**\n461\t         * A proof containing a TSS signature for a single block. This field\n462\t         * SHALL contain the proof of a block with a block number greater\n463\t         * than or equal to the current block; otherwise this field MUST be\n464\t         * omitted.\n465\t         */\n466\t        TssSignedBlockProof signed_block_proof = 2;\n467\t\n468\t        /**\n469\t         * A proof containing RSA signatures from consensus nodes for a record\n470\t         * file. This field MUST contain the proof of the record file only if\n471\t         * the state proof is being provided for a record file; otherwise it\n472\t         * MUST be omitted.\n473\t         */\n474\t        SignedRecordFileProof signed_record_file_proof = 4;\n475\t    }\n476\t}\n477\t\n478\t/**\n479\t * A proof containing RSA signatures from consensus nodes for a record file.\n480\t */\n481\tmessage SignedRecordFileProof {\n482\t    /**\n483\t     * A collection of RSA signatures from consensus nodes.<br/>\n484\t     * These signatures validate the hash of the record_file_contents field.\n485\t     */\n486\t    repeated RecordFileSignature record_file_signatures = 1;\n487\t}\n488\t\n489\t/**\n490\t * A signature by a node on the SHA384 hash of the record file.\n491\t */\n492\tmessage RecordFileSignature {\n493\t    /**\n494\t     * A single RSA signature.<br/>\n495\t     * This is the RSA signature of the node on the SHA384 hash of\n496\t     * the record file\n497\t     */\n498\t    bytes signatures_bytes = 1;\n499\t\n500\t    /**\n501\t     * A unique node identifier.<br/>\n502\t     * This is the node id of the consensus node that created this signature.\n503\t     */\n504\t    int32 node_id = 2;\n505\t}\n506\t```\n507\tThe verification procedure consists of the following steps in order:\n508\t1. Verify the chain-of-trust proof for the `verification_key`; if a `wraps_proof`, this is done using\n509\tthe SNARK verification key.\n510\tpublished in the `LedgerIdPublicationTransactionBody`.\n511\t2. Assert that the SHA-256 hash of `hints_verification_key` equals the value from the proof above.\n512\t3. Use the hinTS verification method to verify `hints_signature` with respect to `hints_verification_key`.\n513\t\n514\t### BlockProof Wire Format\n515\t\n516\tThe `TssSignedBlockProof.block_signature` field is a packed byte array; the field names\n517\treferenced in the verification steps above (`hints_verification_key`, `hints_signature`)\n518\tare byte segments extracted from it, not separate protobuf fields. The encoding is:\n519\t\n520\t```\n521\thints_verification_key (1096 bytes) || hints_signature (1632 bytes) || trailing_segment\n522\t```\n523\t\n524\twhere `trailing_segment` is one of:\n525\t\n526\t| Mode | Trailing segment | Length | Total |\n527\t|---|---|---|---|\n528\t| Normal operation | `wraps_proof` | 704 bytes | 3432 bytes |\n529\t| Bootstrap / genesis | `aggregate_schnorr_signature` | 192 bytes | 2920 bytes |\n530\t\n531\tThe mode is determined solely by the total byte length of `block_signature`; there is\n532\tno explicit mode field.\n533\t\n534\tAll proof artifacts (`hints_verification_key`, `hints_signature`, `wraps_proof`) are\n535\tserialized by ArkWorks Rust libraries. Implementers using non-Rust cryptographic\n536\tlibraries must validate ArkWorks byte encoding against their library's format before\n537\tassuming compatibility. ArkWorks encoding is not guaranteed to match standard library\n538\tdefaults (e.g. BLS12-381 point encoding in `@noble/bls12-381` requires explicit\n539\tconfirmation against ArkWorks output).\n540\t\n541\tThe `aggregate_schnorr_signature` bootstrap mode uses Schnorr signatures on the\n542\t**BabyJubjub** curve (`ark_ed_on_bn254`), with **Blake2s** for challenge hashing and\n543\t**Poseidon** over BN254 Fr for the rotation message hash. Compatibility of\n544\tArkWorks-produced proofs with snarkjs-style verifiers is currently unconfirmed and\n545\trequires validation before use.\n546\t\n547\t## Backwards Compatibility\n548\tBecause we propose to enable hinTS TSS in tandem with the block stream detailed in\n549\t[HIP-1056](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/pull/1056), this\n550\tchange will be one just more part of a sharp and permanent discontinuity in the Hiero \n551\tprotocol. (Hedera may choose to publish TSS block proofs for all historical mainnet\n552\tblocks; but that is not part of the scope of this HIP.)\n553\t\n554\t## Security Implications\n555\tThe attack surface for forging hinTS signatures is essentially the same as already\n556\texists with the V6 record stream today. If an attacker can steal the private keys of\n557\tnodes that hold greater than 1/2 of the stake weight, they can forge network signatures\n558\ton arbitrary blocks (whether that means listing RSA signatures or aggregating BLS\n559\tsignatures). Node operators must therefore remain vigilant and follow best practices\n560\tto ensure the physical and cyber security of their machines.\n561\t\n562\t## How to Teach This\n563\tFor a HIP that adds new functionality or changes interface behaviors, it is\n564\thelpful to include a section on how to teach users, new and experienced, how to\n565\tapply the HIP to their work.\n566\t\n567\t## Reference Implementation\n568\tPlease refer to the `HintsService` and `HistoryService` in the Hiero consensus node\n569\t[repository](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node) for the reference \n570\timplementation.\n571\t\n572\t## Rejected Ideas\n573\tBefore adopting hinTS, there was lengthy consideration of an inexact-weight threshold \n574\tscheme that would approximate each node's stake weight by granting it a number of BLS \n575\tkey pairs called **shares**. Nodes with more stake would get more shares, and nodes \n576\twith less stake would get fewer shares. Although this approach offered at least equal \n577\tor better space and time efficiency than hinTS, the complexity and compromises inherent\n578\twith inexact weights made hinTS the better choice for Hiero networks.\n579\t\n580\t## Open Issues\n581\tNo known issues are currently under discussion.\n582\t\n583\t## References\n584\t\n585\t1. Garg, S., Jain, A., Mukherjee, P., Sinha, R., Wang, M., & Zhang, Y. (2023). *hinTS: Threshold Signatures with Silent\n586\tSetup*. Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2023/567. Retrieved from [https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567](https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567)\n587\t2. [HIP-415: Introduction of Blocks](https://hips.hedera.com/hip/hip-415) \n588\t3. [HIP-1056: Block Streams](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/pull/1056)\n589\t4. [Hiero consensus node](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node) \n590\t5. [Reference implementation design doc](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hedera-node/docs/exact-weight-tss.md)\n591\t6. [SNARK construction state change ordinals](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L430)\n592\t7. [hinTS construction state change ordinals](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L400)\n593\t8. [Ledger id state change ordinal](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L420).\n594\t\n595\t## Copyright/license\n596\tThis document is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 —\n597\tsee [LICENSE](../LICENSE) or <https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0>.\n598\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b68b30f-133f-4078-a9a7-ee7aeeec8953","uuid":"7dcd02cb-cfcd-4b2a-9a37-1f35e0445449","timestamp":"2026-09-02T08:25:49.740Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/hip-1200.md","content":"---\nhip: 1200\ntitle: The hinTS threshold signature scheme \nauthor: Michael Tinker <@tinker-michaelj>, Rohit Sinha <@rsinha> \nworking-group: Edward Wertz <@edward-swirldslabs>, Neeha Sompalli <@Neeharika-Sompalli>\nrequested-by: Hashgraph\ntype: Standards Track\ncategory: Service\nneeds-hiero-approval: Yes\nneeds-hedera-review: Yes\nhedera-review-date: 2025-10-21\nhedera-acceptance-decision: Accepted \nstatus: Approved\nlast-call-date-time: 2025-10-01T07:00:00Z\ncreated: 2025-04-15\ndiscussions-to: https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/discussions/1181\nupdated: 2025-10-15\n---\n\n## Abstract\nThis proposal introduces the hinTS threshold signature scheme (TSS), designed for Hiero networks to sign blocks within the block stream (HIP-1056). It replaces individual RSA signatures with constant-size, efficiently verifiable signatures that are seamlessly integrated into the block stream.\n\nCurrently Hiero networks use record signing in the V6 record stream to ensure integrity, as defined in HIP-415. Each node generates an individual RSA signature on the hash of every block it produces, verified independently by a majority of weighted nodes.\n\nThe current RSA-based signing is inefficient. Verifiers must verify RSA keys from multiple nodes  which is computationally costly and will grow as nodes are added to the network. With uniform stake distribution, signature count and verification work scale linearly with node count. Verifying numerous RSA signatures on EVM chains is impractical due to linear scaling and lack of native RSA precompile support.\n\nThis HIP proposes the adoption of TSS be introduced alongside HIP-1056’s block stream. TSS will produce a constant-size signature per block, simplifying verification for block nodes, mirror nodes and other services, including via EVM smart contracts with gas-efficient precompiles.\n\nTSS provides the following advantages:\nConstant-Size Signatures: Signature size remains fixed, regardless of node count or weight distribution, reducing storage and transmission overhead.\nConstant-Time Verification: Verification work is independent of network size, improving scalability and enabling efficient EVM integration.\n\n## Motivation\nMost consumers of a Hiero network's block stream will need to verify the blocks they receive by checking\nthe network's signature on the block Merkle tree. Many network clients will also want to verify state proofs\nby checking the same signature (since the state Merkle tree is a subtree of the block Merkle tree). It follows\nthat the cost of verifying block signatures has a large effect on a Hiero network's usability, _especially_\nas the network size grows and its history includes many roster and weight changes. For example, suppose a\ndecentralized finance (DeFi) application wants to respond as soon as possible to state changes that\nrepresent onchain market signals. It must be able to verify each block's signature quickly and cheaply.\nAs another example, consider inter-ledger communication between Hiero networks and EVM chains. Any such\ninter-ledger protocol will need to be based on an efficient threshold signature scheme to keep gas costs\nlow.\n\n## Rationale\nThe appeal of the hinTS scheme is described extensively in [1], so we will not repeat each\nreason to adopt it in this HIP. The main benefit we do want to emphasize, other than\nefficiency, is that hinTS lets nodes be assigned **exact weights**, even at the resolution\nof 64-bit stake weights. Moreover, it uses a silent setup, avoiding the communication and \ncomputation heavy protocols often associated with DKG-based threshold signature schemes.\n\nThe support for exact weights is attractive because, for an aBFT proof-of-stake blockchain, the \n**access structure** needed to form a network signature is the agreement of any set of \nnodes holding greater than 1/2 of the total consensus weight. In a Hiero network, consensus \nweights are the whole number of HBARs staked to each node, and the network's HBAR supply\ncan be configured with 64-bit precision. It follows that the weights in a Hiero threshold\naccess structure also need 64-bit precision.\n\nThus, adopting hinTS lets Hiero networks impose exactly the access structure that is\nmost natural for the aggregate signatures of a proof-of-stake blockchain.\n\n## User stories\n- As a Hiero network user, I want to be able to verify the signature of any block proof created \nby the network _without_ keeping the full address book history and the signing (RSA) key of \nevery node throughout that history.\n- As a Hiero network user, I want to do constant-time work to verify each block proof.\n- As a Hiero block node operator, I want concise network signatures to reduce the amount \nof information I need to store and serve to block proof consumers.\n\n## Technical Overview\nThe hinTS signature on any block `N` attests to the statement that the block's content has been signed (via the BLS \nalgorithm) by nodes constituting of greater than 1/2 of the network weight, according to the address book in use for block `N`. \nHowever, since the network can adopt different address books over time, the statement above must also imply that \nthe address book being used in block `N` is a descendent of the genesis address book, wherein each address \nbook rotation is authorized via signatures of greater than 1/2 (in weight) of the members of the active address book.\n\nWe label each address book with a (public) verification key, which is used to verify that the aggregate BLS signature \n(computed by aggregating the BLS partial signatures) is derived from a threshold fraction of the nodes by weight.\nStarting with the genesis address book, each time the network adopts a new address book, it will publish a \nrecursive SNARK proof in the block stream that proves the new address book's verification key belongs to the \nnetwork's **chain of trust** -- the witnesses to this recursive SNARK prover algorithm is the prior recursive \nSNARK proof, and a set of Schnorr signatures from members of the prior address book, representing greater than 1/2 amount of weight.\nTherefore, in hinTS, each block signature contains the following components: \n  1. a BLS aggregate signature, resulting from combining the partial BLS signatures over that block;\n  2. a lightweight SNARK proof proving that the signers in the above BLS aggregate signature comprised of greater than 1/2 of the active \n     address book by weight -- this proof is verified w.r.t. the verification key of the active address book;\n  3. a recursive SNARK proof proving that the active address book is a descendent of the genesis address book;\n\n## Specification\nOur specification has four parts.\n1. First, we define the TSS **address book** and how we can use **proof keys** to link one \naddress book to the next in a cryptographic chain of trust that binds arbitrary **metadata** \nto each address book in the chain. \n2. Next, we outline the information flow in the construction of a hinTS scheme, emphasizing \nthat the main external output of each hinTS scheme, its **verification key**, is exactly the \nmetadata bound to the corresponding address book in the chain of trust.\n3. Third, we discuss the special case of the genesis block that initializes the chain of trust.\n4. Finally, we tie everything together by specifying the `BlockProof` protobuf message a\nHiero network uses to externalize its block signatures.\n\n**Important:** This specification is for developers working on the core protocol, block nodes,\nand mirror nodes. We confine ourselves to just what is needed to correctly produce and consume \nthe TSS protocol outputs in the block stream, taking as given a cryptography library that \nprovides the API of the `HintsLibrary` and `HistoryLibrary` interfaces in the reference \nimplementation in [4]. \n\nReaders interested in thoroughly understanding the reference implementation should refer to \nits [design documentation](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hedera-node/docs/exact-weight-tss.md). \nReaders interested in the deeper details of the cryptography should consult [1].\n\n### Schnorr proof keys and the address book chain of trust\n\nUsers of a Hiero network must apriori trust a large majority of the _originators_ of the network, \nwhich forms the root of trust rather than some cryptographic proof.\nThis is obvious since the originators will, by definition, hold the entire stake weight; \nand, even the gold standard of aBFT security is mathematically limited to a corruption threshold \nof strictly less than a third of the weight.\n\nBut users should never have to extend their trust beyond the aforementioned root \n(and the limits of aBFT fault tolerance and other cryptographic assumptions within hinTS). \nEvery future change to the membership of the network must come with cryptographic proof that at least \none third of the weight (and hence at least one honest node) voted for that membership change.\n\nWe propose a recursive proof mechanism that uses a succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge\n(SNARK) based on honest nodes contributing signatures to address book. These signatures are computed using \nSchnorr private keys whose public keys are gossiped to the network and stored within the address book. \nThe message used to gossip a Schnorr public key is,\n```\nmessage HistoryProofKeyPublicationTransactionBody {\n  /**\n   * The public key the submitting node intends to use when\n   * contributing signatures for use in proving history\n   * belongs to the chain of trust for the ledger id.\n   */\n  bytes history_public_key = 1;\n} \n```\n\nThe network membership is characterized by a **TSS address book** made up of `(nodeId, history_public_key, weight)` \ntriples; where the `history_public_key`s are the Schnorr public keys gossiped as above; \nand the `nodeId` and `weight` fields are identical to those in a current or candidate consensus roster.\n\nIt follows that every recursive proof construction derives from a **source roster** (and address book), \nand aims to prove a trustworthy transition to a **target roster** (and address book). The first \ntime that TSS is enabled on a Hiero network is a special case in which the same roster serves as \nboth source _and_ target; the TSS address book for this special roster is called the **genesis \naddress book**. From that point forward, the SHA-256 hash of the genesis address book is the\n **ledger id**.\n```\nledgerId = <genesisAddressBookHash>\n```\n\nIn the reference implementation, as soon as the ledger id is computed, it is set in a singleton\nwith the State API key `HistoryService.LEDGER_ID` with `StateIdentifier` ordinal\n[`42`](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L420).\nThis singleton has the simple type,\n```\nmessage ProtoBytes {\n    bytes value = 1;\n}\n```\n\nFor record stream legibility, when the ledger id is created the network also publishes a\nnew synthetic transaction body,\n```\nmessage LedgerIdPublicationTransactionBody {\n  /**\n   * The new ledger id.\n   */\n  bytes ledger_id = 1;\n\n  /**\n   * The key to use when verifying recursive chain-of-trust proofs that extend\n   * from this ledger id.\n   */\n  bytes history_proof_verification_key = 2;\n}\n```\nwhere the `history_proof_verification_key` is the key used to verify the recursive proofs\nthat extend the chain of trust from the ledger id through TSS address book changes.\n\nAny block stream consumer that wants to validate signatures should keep the ledger id\nreadily available, since it is an ingredient in every chain-of-trust proof.\n\nThe honest nodes in the network prove every subsequent address book change \n--- with respect to the above ledger id --- by first using a deterministic \npolicy to determine the hash of the target address book, then gossiping their \nSchnorr signatures on the hash of that address book after being\n_concatenated with arbitrary metadata that should be trusted for that address book_. \nThe message used to gossip these signatures is,\n```\nmessage HistoryProofSignatureTransactionBody {\n  /**\n   * The id of the proof construction this signature is contributing to.\n   */\n  uint64 construction_id = 1;\n\n  /**\n   * This node's signature on its computed history.\n   */\n  com.hedera.hapi.node.state.history.HistorySignature signature = 2;\n}\n```\n\nWhere we have,\n```\n/**\n * A piece of new history in the form of an address book hash and\n * associated metadata.\n */\nmessage History {\n  /**\n   * The address book hash of the new history.\n   */\n  bytes address_book_hash = 1;\n  /**\n   * The metadata associated to the address book.\n   */\n  bytes metadata = 2;\n}\n\n/**\n * A node's signature blessing some new history.\n */\nmessage HistorySignature {\n  /**\n   * The new history the node is signing.\n   */\n  History history = 1;\n  /**\n   * The node's signature on the canonical serialization of\n   * the new history.\n   */\n  bytes signature = 2;\n}\n```\n\nThe honest nodes then use another deterministic policy to take a sufficient set of the\nvalid signatures as inputs to the recursive SNARK prover; compute the proof; and then \ngossip their vote for that proof as the evidence the roster transition is trustworthy.\n\nThe arbitrary metadata concatenated with the address book is the bridge between the\naddress book chain of trust and the hinTS signing scheme actually used to create TSS\nsignatures, as we see next.\n\n### hinTS BLS keys and TSS signatures\n\nWhen faced with a new candidate address book, the honest nodes in the network must\nwork together to setup the hinTS scheme for that address book. The first step\nis to gossip a special, cryptographically \"extended\" form of the public BLS key \n-- called the hinTS key -- that they have generated for their individual use.\n```\nmessage HintsKeyPublicationTransactionBody {\n  ...\n  /**\n   * The party's hinTS key.\n   */\n  bytes hints_key = 3;\n}\n```\n\nUnlike simple BLS keys, these extended hinTS keys have information that can be **preprocessed** \ninto an aggregated **verification key**. Given a verification key `VK` that aggregates the \nhinTS keys `{ k_1, k_2, ..., k_n }`, the hinTS scheme lets us replace (1) a linear-size list \nof partial signatures `{ s_1, s_2, ... s_n }` that verify under the respective keys; \nwith (2) a single aggregate signature `S` (with claimed aggregate weight) that verifies under `VK`.\n\nThe honest nodes use a deterministic policy to choose which of the published hinTS\nkeys they will aggregate into the verification key for the target address book, then\ngossip a vote on the resulting preprocessing output. The honest nodes adopt the `VK`\nfrom the preprocessing output that receives votes from at least a third of the stake\nweight in the source address book for the construction.\n\n### The genesis block\n\nWe discussed above how the `VK` for the hinTS scheme of an address book is included\nin the chain-of-trust proof that links that address book to the previous one. However,\nchain must have a beginning---that is, a genesis address book with an initial hinTS\nscheme.\n\nFor the genesis hinTS `VK`, there is no recursive proof. The genesis block itself\nmust be trusted. Specifically, the Hiero protocol guarantees the genesis block of\na network will include,\n1. A `HistoryProofKeyPublicationTransactionBody` for strictly more than 2/3 of the\nweight in the genesis roster.\n2. The ledger id derived from the hash of the address book with these keys.\n3. A `HistoryProofSignatureTransactionBody` on the `History` formed from this\nledger id and the genesis hinTS `VK` for greater than 1/2 of the weight in the genesis\nroster.\n\nIf a stream consumer trusts it knows the ledger id for a network, it can then,\ngiven the genesis block, verify the Schnorr keys and roster in that block hash\nto the ledger id. Since this proves the Schnorr keys are trustworthy, their\nsignatures in the genesis block then establish the genesis hinTS key.\n\n### Verifying `BlockProof`s as a stream consumer\n\nA Hiero network externalizes the proof of each block in the following message.\n```\nmessage BlockProof {\n    /**\n     * The block this proof secures.<br/>\n     * We provide this because a proof for a future block can be used to prove\n     * the state of the ledger at that block and the blocks before it.<br/>\n     * <p>\n     * This value SHOULD match the block number of the current block,\n     * under normal operation.\n     */\n    uint64 block = 1;\n\n    /**\n     * The hinTS key that this signature verifies under; a stream consumer should\n     * only use this key after first checking the chain of trust proof.\n     */\n    bytes verification_key = 2;\n\n    /**\n     * Proof the hinTS verification key is in the chain of trust extending\n     * from the network's ledger id.\n     */\n    ChainOfTrustProof verification_key_proof = 3;\n\n    /**\n     * The proof contents verifying the block's merkle root hash.<br/>\n     * This is a `oneof` field that MAY contain one several types of\n     * proofs.\n     */\n    oneof proof {\n        /**\n         * A TSS signature over the block's merkle root hash.<br/>\n         * This signature SHALL be produced by a threshold signature scheme\n         * that allows multiple nodes to contribute partial signatures that\n         * can be aggregated into a single signature. This field MUST be\n         * used when the current block is signed directly by the consensus\n         * nodes with a TSS signature; otherwise it MUST be empty.\n         */\n        TssSignedBlockProof signed_block_proof = 4;\n\n        /**\n         * A proof of the block merkle tree's contents. This proof SHALL\n         * contain the information necessary to validate the previous block's\n         * hash, along with any information necessary to validate the current\n         * block's hash.\n         * <p>\n         * Of necessity, the state proof MUST contain a set of hash values,\n         * along with ordering information, that allows for reconstruction of the\n         * block's hash. This list of hash values form the set of sibling hash values\n         * needed to correctly reconstruct the parent hash, and all hash values\n         * \"above\" that hash in the merkle tree.\n         * <p>\n         * A Block proof can be constructed by combining the sibling hashes for\n         * a previous block hash and sibling hashes for each entry \"above\" that\n         * node in the merkle tree of a block proof that incorporates that previous\n         * block hash. This form of block proof may be used to prove a chain of\n         * blocks when one or more older blocks is missing the original block\n         * proof that signed the block's merkle root directly.\n         * <p>\n         * Such a list MUST be ordered from the sibling of the node that contains\n         * this block's root node hash, and continues up the merkle tree to the\n         * root hash of the signed block proof.\n         * <p>\n         * If this block proof has a \"direct\" signature, then any associated list\n         * of siblings MUST be empty.<br/>\n         * If said list is not empty, then this block proof MUST be verified by\n         * first constructing the \"block\" merkle tree and computing the root hash\n         * of that tree, then combining that hash with the values in this list,\n         * paying attention to the first/second sibling ordering, until the root\n         * merkle hash is produced from the last pair of sibling hashes. That\n         * \"secondary\" root hash MUST then be verified using the value of\n         * `block_signature`.\n         */\n        StateProof block_state_proof = 5;\n\n        /**\n         * A proof consisting of RSA signatures from consensus nodes.<br/>\n         * This proof type exists for backward compatibility with blocks that\n         * wrap historical record files.<br/>\n         * This field MUST be set when the block wraps a record file signed by\n         * individual RSA signatures from consensus nodes; otherwise it MUST be\n         * empty.\n         */\n        SignedRecordFileProof signed_record_file_proof = 6;\n    }\n}\n\nmessage ChainOfTrustProof {\n    oneof proof {\n        /**\n         * If there is not yet a SNARK proving the chain of trust from ledger id to\n         * the hinTS verification key, an aggregation of Schnorr signatures on\n         * the concatenation of the ledger id and genesis hinTS verification key\n         * that serve as witnesses for the SNARK prover algorithm.\n         */\n        AggregatedNodeSignatures aggregated_node_signatures = 1;\n        /**\n         * If known, a ZK-compressed SNARK proof proving the chain of trust from\n         * the ledger id to this hinTS verification key.\n         */\n        bytes wraps_proof = 2;\n    }\n}\n\n/**\n * An aggregation of node signatures on some data.\n * <p>\n * Can be used to prove the genesis hinTS verification key in a block proof; but\n * not succinct and not recursive; hence in normal operations with TSS, used only\n * until the first recursive proof is available.\n */\nmessage AggregatedNodeSignatures {\n  /**\n   * The aggregated signature.\n   */\n  bytes aggregated_signature = 1;\n  /**\n   * In ascending order, the ids of the nodes that contributed signatures.\n   */\n  repeated uint64 signing_node_ids = 2;\n}\n\n/**\n * A proof containing a TSS signature for a single block.\n */\nmessage TssSignedBlockProof {\n    /**\n     * A TSS signature for one block.<br/>\n     * This is a single signature representing the collection of partial\n     * signatures from nodes holding strictly greater than 2/3 of the\n     * current network \"weight\" in aggregate. The signature is produced by\n     * cryptographic \"aggregation\" of the partial signatures to produce a\n     * single signature that can be verified with the network public key,\n     * but could not be produced by fewer nodes than required to meet the\n     * threshold for network stake \"weight\".\n     * <p>\n     * This message MUST make use of a threshold signature scheme like `BLS`\n     * which provides the necessary cryptographic guarantees.<br/>\n     * This signature SHALL use a TSS signature to provide a single signature\n     * that represents the consensus signature of consensus nodes.<br/>\n     * The exact subset of nodes that signed SHALL neither be known nor\n     * tracked, but it SHALL be cryptographically verifiable that the\n     * threshold was met if the signature itself can be validated with\n     * the network public key (a.k.a `LedgerID`).\n     */\n    bytes block_signature = 1;\n}\n\nmessage StateProof {\n    /**\n     * The merkle paths that prove the nodes in the block merkle tree.\n     * This field SHALL contain, in the following order:\n     * - The merkle path from the previous block root (the \"left-most\"\n     *   node on level 5) to it's (single) internal node parent (on level\n     *   1, adjacent to the block's consensus timestamp).\n     * - The merkle path from the consensus timestamp leaf to the root.\n     * - The merkle path of the root of the entire block merkle tree.\n     */\n    repeated MerklePath paths = 1;\n\n    oneof proof {\n        /**\n         * A proof containing a TSS signature for a single block. This field\n         * SHALL contain the proof of a block with a block number greater\n         * than or equal to the current block; otherwise this field MUST be\n         * omitted.\n         */\n        TssSignedBlockProof signed_block_proof = 2;\n\n        /**\n         * A proof containing RSA signatures from consensus nodes for a record\n         * file. This field MUST contain the proof of the record file only if\n         * the state proof is being provided for a record file; otherwise it\n         * MUST be omitted.\n         */\n        SignedRecordFileProof signed_record_file_proof = 4;\n    }\n}\n\n/**\n * A proof containing RSA signatures from consensus nodes for a record file.\n */\nmessage SignedRecordFileProof {\n    /**\n     * A collection of RSA signatures from consensus nodes.<br/>\n     * These signatures validate the hash of the record_file_contents field.\n     */\n    repeated RecordFileSignature record_file_signatures = 1;\n}\n\n/**\n * A signature by a node on the SHA384 hash of the record file.\n */\nmessage RecordFileSignature {\n    /**\n     * A single RSA signature.<br/>\n     * This is the RSA signature of the node on the SHA384 hash of\n     * the record file\n     */\n    bytes signatures_bytes = 1;\n\n    /**\n     * A unique node identifier.<br/>\n     * This is the node id of the consensus node that created this signature.\n     */\n    int32 node_id = 2;\n}\n```\nThe verification procedure consists of the following steps in order:\n1. Verify the chain-of-trust proof for the `verification_key`; if a `wraps_proof`, this is done using\nthe SNARK verification key.\npublished in the `LedgerIdPublicationTransactionBody`.\n2. Assert that the SHA-256 hash of `hints_verification_key` equals the value from the proof above.\n3. Use the hinTS verification method to verify `hints_signature` with respect to `hints_verification_key`.\n\n### BlockProof Wire Format\n\nThe `TssSignedBlockProof.block_signature` field is a packed byte array; the field names\nreferenced in the verification steps above (`hints_verification_key`, `hints_signature`)\nare byte segments extracted from it, not separate protobuf fields. The encoding is:\n\n```\nhints_verification_key (1096 bytes) || hints_signature (1632 bytes) || trailing_segment\n```\n\nwhere `trailing_segment` is one of:\n\n| Mode | Trailing segment | Length | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Normal operation | `wraps_proof` | 704 bytes | 3432 bytes |\n| Bootstrap / genesis | `aggregate_schnorr_signature` | 192 bytes | 2920 bytes |\n\nThe mode is determined solely by the total byte length of `block_signature`; there is\nno explicit mode field.\n\nAll proof artifacts (`hints_verification_key`, `hints_signature`, `wraps_proof`) are\nserialized by ArkWorks Rust libraries. Implementers using non-Rust cryptographic\nlibraries must validate ArkWorks byte encoding against their library's format before\nassuming compatibility. ArkWorks encoding is not guaranteed to match standard library\ndefaults (e.g. BLS12-381 point encoding in `@noble/bls12-381` requires explicit\nconfirmation against ArkWorks output).\n\nThe `aggregate_schnorr_signature` bootstrap mode uses Schnorr signatures on the\n**BabyJubjub** curve (`ark_ed_on_bn254`), with **Blake2s** for challenge hashing and\n**Poseidon** over BN254 Fr for the rotation message hash. Compatibility of\nArkWorks-produced proofs with snarkjs-style verifiers is currently unconfirmed and\nrequires validation before use.\n\n## Backwards Compatibility\nBecause we propose to enable hinTS TSS in tandem with the block stream detailed in\n[HIP-1056](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/pull/1056), this\nchange will be one just more part of a sharp and permanent discontinuity in the Hiero \nprotocol. (Hedera may choose to publish TSS block proofs for all historical mainnet\nblocks; but that is not part of the scope of this HIP.)\n\n## Security Implications\nThe attack surface for forging hinTS signatures is essentially the same as already\nexists with the V6 record stream today. If an attacker can steal the private keys of\nnodes that hold greater than 1/2 of the stake weight, they can forge network signatures\non arbitrary blocks (whether that means listing RSA signatures or aggregating BLS\nsignatures). Node operators must therefore remain vigilant and follow best practices\nto ensure the physical and cyber security of their machines.\n\n## How to Teach This\nFor a HIP that adds new functionality or changes interface behaviors, it is\nhelpful to include a section on how to teach users, new and experienced, how to\napply the HIP to their work.\n\n## Reference Implementation\nPlease refer to the `HintsService` and `HistoryService` in the Hiero consensus node\n[repository](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node) for the reference \nimplementation.\n\n## Rejected Ideas\nBefore adopting hinTS, there was lengthy consideration of an inexact-weight threshold \nscheme that would approximate each node's stake weight by granting it a number of BLS \nkey pairs called **shares**. Nodes with more stake would get more shares, and nodes \nwith less stake would get fewer shares. Although this approach offered at least equal \nor better space and time efficiency than hinTS, the complexity and compromises inherent\nwith inexact weights made hinTS the better choice for Hiero networks.\n\n## Open Issues\nNo known issues are currently under discussion.\n\n## References\n\n1. Garg, S., Jain, A., Mukherjee, P., Sinha, R., Wang, M., & Zhang, Y. (2023). *hinTS: Threshold Signatures with Silent\nSetup*. Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2023/567. Retrieved from [https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567](https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/567)\n2. [HIP-415: Introduction of Blocks](https://hips.hedera.com/hip/hip-415) \n3. [HIP-1056: Block Streams](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-improvement-proposals/pull/1056)\n4. [Hiero consensus node](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node) \n5. [Reference implementation design doc](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hedera-node/docs/exact-weight-tss.md)\n6. [SNARK construction state change ordinals](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L430)\n7. [hinTS construction state change ordinals](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L400)\n8. [Ledger id state change ordinal](https://github.com/hiero-ledger/hiero-consensus-node/blob/main/hapi/hedera-protobuf-java-api/src/main/proto/block/stream/output/state_changes.proto#L420).\n\n## Copyright/license\nThis document is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 